\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Delays Delivery Of New 777X Jet\b0\par\par\uc2 By Drew FitzGerald \par \uc2 686 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said Wednesday it pushed back the first delivery of its new 777X model to 2027 after falling behind schedule on some certification milestones.\par \par \uc2 The company booked a $4.9 billion noncash charge in the third quarter to account for the new setback.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said it is making more of its workhorse 737 MAX jets with fewer restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "We very much underestimated how much work it was going to take" to secure the needed approvals for the 777X, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  said on a conference call with analysts. "I'm certainly not throwing the \uc2 FAA\uc2  under the bus with this. This is a learning collectively for the both of us."\par \par \uc2 Ortberg described the setback as partly a paperwork problem and said the air frame and engines continued to perform well after more than 4,000 hours of flight tests.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  shares dropped 4.3% on Wednesday, after the accounting charge topped Wall Street analysts' projections.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg warned in a September investor conference that the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  was taking longer to certify the wide-body 777X than the company would like.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  faces a steep order backlog for the long-distance jet, which executives had initially promised to airlines as early as 2020.\par \par \uc2 Each new delay in shipping the aircraft lengthens the time it will take \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to recoup its investments in the long-delayed 777X program.\par \par \uc2 Some customers and suppliers could also demand payments for the schedule slip.\par \par \uc2 The 777X charge overshadowed gradual improvements in other business lines. The company delivered 160 commercial aircraft during the quarter, another improvement over last year's depressed levels.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  last month started loosening its grip on the production process for two in-production \uc2 Boeing\uc2  jets, the 737 MAX and 787 Dreamliner, giving company workers authority to approve some safety checks.\par \par \uc2 The regulator will continue to directly handle the issuance of airworthiness certificates needed for each aircraft to fly passengers.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. regulator this month also allowed \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to ramp up its 737 MAX output to 42 jets a month from its earlier 38-plane pace.\par \par \uc2 That milestone was key to the company's effort to catch up with mounting orders for the narrow-body jet.\par \par \uc2 U.S. oversight of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  intensified in 2019 after two fatal 737 MAX crashes.\par \par \uc2 Regulators throttled the company's jet production rate after a midair panel blowout on a 2024 \uc2 Alaska Air\uc2  flight exposed persistent quality-control issues.\par \par \uc2 "Our team continues to work under \uc2 FAA\uc2  oversight to ensure every airplane meets all safety and certification standards," Ortberg wrote in a Wednesday letter to employees.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reported a $5.34 billion loss over the third quarter, which included the 777X impact. Revenue jumped 30% to $23.3 billion, reflecting surging commercial-jet deliveries.\par \par \uc2 The company also ended the quarter generating more cash than it burned. It reported $238 million of free cash flow, its first positive quarterly result since 2023.\par \par \uc2 The company's defense division, which covers weapons, jet fighters and spacecraft, swung to an operating profit as revenue jumped 25% to $6.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 The company said a new satellite contract and swelling demand for its Patriot missile interceptor components were improving the division's prospects.\par \par \uc2 The quarter included nearly two months of work stoppage in \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's St. Louis-area plants, where about 3,200 of the company's defense workers have been on strike since early August.\par \par \uc2 Steve Parker\uc2 , chief of the aerospace company's defense segment, said in a Tuesday letter to workers that the company wouldn't move off its basic contract terms and urged them to cross the picket line.\par \par \uc2 The union local that represents the plant workers said late Tuesday it had filed a second unfair-labor-practices complaint to federal regulators over the company's "refusal to bargain in good faith."\par \par \uc2 "Every American should be deeply concerned that this company is prolonging this strike and attempting to hire third-party workers without our experience to build some of the most advanced military jets and defense systems in the world," the union said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich325\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4875
FF
