\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Shutdown Hits Elite Travelers --- Shortage of airport workers creates snarls for exclusive screening lanes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Passy \par \uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 First-class passengers flying out of New York and Los Angeles may feel like they are getting coach treatment thanks to the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Exclusive screening lanes for Delta One customers at New York's John F. Kennedy International Airport and \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  aren't currently active, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  confirmed. These lanes normally allow passengers who buy pricey Delta One airfare for international flights from those airports to bypass the standard security checkpoints.\par \par \uc2 On Delta's website, the airline says the upgrade makes travel "feel effortless from the moment you arrive." Now, those customers are being forced to go through standard security lanes at airports alongside everyone else.\par \par \uc2 Delta One service isn't cheap: One such ticket for a flight from JFK to Paris-Charles de Gaulle Airport this Friday cost more than $4,000 as of Tuesday evening. "We're working with our TSA partners in these locations to have customers screened through other checkpoints," a Delta spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 The special access shut down is just one of many indignities piling up as travelers face week five of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown. Federal officials have warned that an extended shutdown is further straining America's already Balkanized, understaffed air travel infrastructure -- from agents who screen passengers to air-traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 Delta noted it has a longstanding partnership with TSA where trained airline employees will assist at the airline's special checkpoints to expedite the process. The carrier has also arranged for a limited number of meals to be provided to transportation-sector employees who are working without pay at this point.\par \par \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  both said their concierge services, which include expedited security for VIP passengers, haven't seen any impact from the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 United said that its Premier Access priority security lanes, which are available at 60 airports globally, including major U.S. hubs like Los Angeles and Chicago's O'Hare International Airport, are still available to eligible travelers amid the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 United's special security lanes are available to a wider range of passengers than Delta's, such as those with status in the MileagePlus frequent-flier program, travelers flying in business and first class, certain credit-card holders and those who purchase Premier Access benefits.\par \par \uc2 The longer the shutdown lasts, the more likely it is that lower-paid essential workers like the TSA employees who oversee airport security checkpoints will opt to call out of work. Travelers are more likely to encounter longer lines as a result.\par \par \uc2 "While the vast majority of TSA's nationwide operations remain minimally impacted by the government shutdown, occasional delays at some security checkpoints are to be expected," a TSA spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 In addition, dozens of staffing shortages at air-traffic control facilities have prompted the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  to slow plane traffic at airports across the country for several weeks, contributing to thousands of flight delays.\par \par \uc2 There were 22 "staffing triggers" at air-traffic control sites across the country on Saturday alone, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2  said on Fox News program "Sunday Morning Futures."\par \par \uc2 "You can see the stress," Duffy said of air-traffic controllers. "These are people that oftentimes live paycheck to paycheck." Tuesday of this week was the first full paycheck that air-traffic controllers missed since the shutdown began, he said.\par \par \uc2 Staffing triggers occur when the number of personnel at a given facility is below optimal levels and can lead to the \uc2 FAA\uc2  choosing to slow or stop air traffic.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 National Air Traffic Controllers Association\uc2  spokesman said it "has consistently warned that the controller staffing shortage leaves the system vulnerable, and the recent events underscore the urgent need to accelerate hiring and training."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251030elau00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
