\par\par \b \uc2 UPS Discloses 48,000 Job Cuts, Reports Decline in Quarterly Profit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung and Connor Hart \par \uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  has cut 48,000 management and operations positions, the company disclosed Tuesday when it reported earnings.\par \par \uc2 Of those, 14,000 were management positions and 34,000 were jobs in operations. The reductions came through layoffs and buyouts, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The cuts were greater than the shipper announced in April when it said it would cut about 20,000 operational jobs. In January 2024, UPS said it planned to cut about 12,000 management jobs.\par \par \uc2 UPS said it is well-positioned to navigate the holiday shipping season, and that restructuring efforts have resulted in cost savings of about $2.2 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "We keep finding opportunities for us to bring costs down," Chief Executive \uc2 Carol Tome\uc2  said. "We are positioned to run the most efficient peak in our history."\par \par \uc2 Tome has been under pressure to reverse a long slump in the company's stock price. The pressure stems particularly from employees and retirees, who have an outsize say under the company's unique shareholder structure, The Wall Street Journal previously reported. Many UPS workers and retirees own supervoting shares that give them 63% of the voting power.\par \par \uc2 Tome is the first outsider to lead UPS. She took over in the middle of the Covid-19 pandemic and revamped the business model to focus on shipping more-profitable packages rather than on volume gains, according to the Journal.\par \par \uc2 Though third-quarter profit and revenue fell, results were better than expected. The company expects fourth-quarter revenue above Wall Street's forecast.\par \par \uc2 International package revenue climbed 5.9% to $4.67 billion, boosted by an increase in average daily volumes. The gain was offset by supply-chain solutions revenue, which fell 22% to $2.52 billion, hurt by the divestiture of Coyote last year.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose 8% Tuesday to $96.36. Through Monday's close, the stock has lost more than one-quarter of its value since the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 The company posted profit of $1.31 billion, or $1.55 a share, compared with $1.54 billion, or $1.80 a share, a year earlier. The quarter included about $250 million in charges related to restructuring efforts. Stripping out certain one-time items, earnings were $1.74 a share. Analysts polled by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  were expecting adjusted earnings of $1.29 a share.\par \par \uc2 Revenue slipped 3.7% to $21.42 billion but came in ahead of the $20.84 billion that Wall Street had modeled.\par \par \uc2 U.S. domestic revenue slipped 2.6% to $14.22 billion, primarily due to an expected decline in volume. However, this was partially offset by higher revenue per piece and air cargo revenue.\par \par \uc2 As of Sept. 30, UPS said it has realized cost savings of about $2.2 billion. The company expects to achieve $3.5 billion total year-over-year cost savings in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The staff revamp came after UPS said it was cutting volumes from \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , which at the time was its biggest customer, The Wall Street Journal reported. UPS added that it expects its average daily volume during the peak season to be lower this year due to its plan for lower volume from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the current quarter, which includes the holiday season, UPS expects revenue of about $24 billion.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich309\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4635
