\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Federal Workers Union Pressures Democrats on Shutdown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anvee Bhutani, Siobhan Hughes and Lindsay Wise \par \uc2 495 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The nation's largest federal workers union called for Congress to end the shutdown, now in its fourth week, putting new pressure on Senate Democrats who have repeatedly blocked a Republican measure to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 "It's time to pass a clean continuing resolution and end this shutdown today," said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , referring to a short-term spending bill. The \uc2 AFGE\uc2  represents more than 800,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 Kelley called the situation an "avoidable crisis" that is harming families and communities. "Both political parties have made their point, and still there is no clear end in sight," he said.\par \par \uc2 The union's demand -- that the Senate pass a continuing resolution with no contentious elements attached -- is the same approach the Republicans have urged Democrats to adopt for the past month, though \uc2 AFGE\uc2  didn't mention either party by name. The union didn't immediately reply to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands of government employees -- whether working or furloughed -- missed full paychecks last week, and more are set to go without pay this week. Those include workers in high-pressure jobs like that of air-traffic controller, in which absenteeism could lead to snarls in the nation's airports. Workers get back pay after a shutdown ends, but meanwhile are turning to savings, short-term loans and even food banks to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders have said that the only way out of the shutdown is to pass a continuing resolution -- a stopgap measure funding the government -- and then turn to any negotiations. But Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) has demanded bipartisan negotiations to extend expiring Affordable Care Act healthcare subsidies before Democrats will vote to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 A number of big deadlines are approaching. Open enrollment for \uc2 ACA\uc2  users begins on Nov. 1, and the Agriculture Department has warned that it won't be able to fund the SNAP food-aid program, which has 40 million users, next month.\par \par \uc2 Democrats acknowledged \uc2 AFGE\uc2 's statement but said it didn't change their mind on the Republican bill.\par \par \uc2 "It's a hard calculus to figure out," said Sen. John Hickenlooper (D., Colo.), who said he remained a no on the GOP proposal. "I can't remember a situation where there were less palatable choices."\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats have repeatedly rejected the GOP's stopgap plan, which passed the House last month and would reopen the government until Nov. 21. No alternative path has emerged in the Senate, where Republicans have a 53-47 majority but 60 votes are needed to advance most legislation. The House remains out of session, as part of a Republican effort to block any other options.\par \par \uc2 House Majority Whip \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  (R., Minn.) told reporters Monday that he was "glad to see" the union was calling to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251028elas0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
