\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Defense Workers Reject Latest Contract --- St. Louis-area machinists have been on strike since early August\b0\par\par\uc2 By Drew FitzGerald \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Roughly 3,200 striking St. Louis-area \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers will stay on the picket line after members rejected a modified contract proposal for a fourth time.\par \par \uc2 The local IAM 837 union said its members voted down the aerospace companys new five-year contract proposal. The company said its proposal failed by a 51%-to-49% vote. Workers at Boeings St. Louis-area plants help assemble jet fighters, trainer aircraft and bomb kits.\par \par \uc2 Union representatives have called \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's previous offers insulting and pointed to recent wage gains by workers in the company's Northwest commercial operations. The St. Louis factories work on jet fighters at a relatively lower volume, but area production is expected to surge as the company prepares to build its next-generation F-47 Air Force jets in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Boeing\uc2  can't keep playing favorites between regions and expect our members to accept less," Jody Bennett, IAM resident general vice president, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The St. Louis machinists walked off the job in early August after narrowly rejecting an earlier four-year contract proposal.\par \par \uc2 In September, they rejected a third proposal that included a $4,000 lump-sum signing bonus. The most recent proposal, which wasn't endorsed by the union, added new restricted-stock and retention awards while lowering some other progression-based wage figures.\par \par \uc2 The St. Louis workers are striking after a contract was reached last year for 33,000 machinists who assemble \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial aircraft in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 Those Seattle-area workers, frustrated by years of stagnant real wages and the soaring cost of living, went on strike for nearly eight weeks. The walk-off yielded them a 38% wage increase over four years and a $12,000 signing bonus.\par \par \uc2 The St. Louis-area union represents a smaller share of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's workforce than its commercial lines in Washington state. The manufacturer has leaned on managers in recent weeks to keep production on track for weaponry like Joint Direct Attack Munitions, or JDAMS.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  told an investor conference in September that the St. Louis strike had slowed some work on F-15 and F-18 jet fighters. Production of JDAMS continued apace during the strike.\par \par \uc2 Steve Parker\uc2 , the head of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's defense business, said in a letter to workers earlier this month that the company was willing to make modifications without budging on the overall contract's value.\par \par \uc2 "This strike is costing you, and more time on the picket line won't result in more money," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251027elar0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
