\par\par \b \uc2 Companies Bet They Can Grow Without Hiring\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 855 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's the corporate gamble of the moment: Can you run a company, increasing sales and juicing profits, without adding people?\par \par \uc2 American employers are increasingly making the calculation that they can keep the size of their teams flat -- or shrink through layoffs -- without harming their businesses. Part of that thinking is the belief that artificial intelligence will be used to pick up some of the slack and automate more processes. Companies also are hesitant to make any moves in an economy many still describe as uncertain.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's chief financial officer told investors recently that the bank now has a "very strong bias against having the reflective response" to hire more people for any given need. Aerospace and defense company RTX boasted last week that its sales rose even without adding employees.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , meanwhile, sent a memo to staffers this month saying the firm "will constrain head count growth through the end of the year" and reduce roles that could be more efficient with AI. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the nation's largest private employer, also said it plans to keep its head count roughly flat over the next three years, even as its sales grow.\par \par \uc2 "If people are getting more productive, you don't need to hire more people," \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 , \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 's chief executive, said in an interview. "I see a lot of companies pre-emptively holding the line, forecasting and hoping that they can have smaller workforces."\par \par \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  employs around 7,000 people, and Chesky says he doesn't expect that number to grow much over the next year. With the help of AI, he said he hopes that "the team we already have can get considerably more work done."\par \par \uc2 Many companies seem intent on embracing a new, ultra-lean model of staffing, one in which more roles are kept unfilled and hiring is treated as a last resort. At \uc2 Intuit\uc2 , every time a job comes open, managers are pushed to justify why they need to backfill it, said \uc2 Sandeep Aujla\uc2 , the company's chief financial officer. The new rigor around hiring helps combat corporate bloat.\par \par \uc2 "That typical behavior that settles in -- and we're all guilty of it -- is, historically, if someone leaves, if Jane Doe leaves, I've got to backfill Jane," Aujla said in an interview. Now, when someone quits, the company asks: "Is there an opportunity for us to rethink how we staff?"\par \par \uc2 Intuit\uc2  has chosen not to replace certain roles in its finance, legal and customer-support functions, he said. In its latest fiscal year, the company's revenue rose 16% even as its head count stayed flat, and it is planning only modest hiring in the current year.\par \par \uc2 The desire to avoid hiring or filling jobs reflects a growing push among executives to see a return on their AI spending. On earnings calls, mentions of ROI and AI investments are increasing, according to an analysis by \uc2 AlphaSense\uc2 , reflecting heightened interest from analysts and investors that companies make good on the millions they are pouring into AI.\par \par \uc2 Many executives hope that software coding assistants and armies of digital agents will keep improving -- even if the current results still at times leave something to be desired.\par \par \uc2 The widespread caution in hiring now is frustrating job seekers and leading many employees within organizations to feel stuck in place, unable to ascend or take on new roles, workers and bosses say.\par \par \uc2 Inside many large companies, HR chiefs also say it is becoming increasingly difficult to predict just how many employees will be needed as technology takes on more of the work.\par \par \uc2 Some employers seem to think that fewer employees will actually improve operations.\par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  last week said it is cutting 600 jobs in its AI division, a move some leaders hailed as a way to cut down on bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 "By reducing the size of our team, fewer conversations will be required to make a decision, and each person will be more load-bearing and have more scope and impact," \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief AI officer, wrote in a memo to staff seen by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Though layoffs haven't been widespread through the economy, some companies are making cuts. Target on Thursday said it would cut about 1,000 corporate employees, and close another 800 open positions, totaling around 8% of its corporate workforce. \uc2 Michael Fiddelke\uc2 , \uc2 Target\uc2 's incoming CEO, said in a memo sent to staff that too "many layers and overlapping work have slowed decisions, making it harder to bring ideas to life."\par \par \uc2 A range of other employers, from electric-truck maker \uc2 Rivian\uc2  to cable and broadband provider \uc2 Charter Communications\uc2 , have announced their own staff cuts in recent weeks, too.\par \par \uc2 Operating with fewer people can still pose risks for companies by straining existing staffers or hurting efforts to develop future leaders, executives and economists say. "It's a bit of a double-edged sword," said Matthew Martin, senior U.S. economist at Oxford Economics. "You want to keep your head count costs down now -- but you also have to have an eye on the future."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6675
