\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Markets: \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2  Cuts Jobs, Fiscal-Year Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 By Connor Hart \par \uc2 452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2  slashed its outlook for the year and trimmed its workforce further as it faces a prolonged and severe slowdown in government-contract funding, particularly in its civil sector.\par \par \uc2 "The reacceleration of our business will take longer than we expected," Chief Executive \uc2 Horacio Rozanski\uc2  said Friday. "I am disappointed in our results this quarter, and that we are lowering guidance across all key metrics."\par \par \uc2 Shares closed 8.86% lower, at $91.40, after the company posted lower profit and revenue in its fiscal second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2  said it ended the recent quarter with roughly 33,000 employees, with customer-facing staff down about 3% sequentially and 10% from last year. Those declines are steeper than the 7% of its workforce the company said it planned to trim in May.\par \par \uc2 Finance Chief \uc2 Matt Calderone\uc2  said the cuts "largely reflect lingering effects from contract run-rate reductions in our civil business, as well as deliberate actions to improve utilization of existing staff."\par \par \uc2 The company also lowered its outlook to reflect a stark contrast between its two main government portfolios. The company's civil business is operating in the most challenging market in a generation, Rozanski said, noting that contracts have been cut as funding has stalled.\par \par \uc2 The outlook for the business isn't improving, the CEO added. \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2  anticipates revenue from its civil business will fall in the low-20% range this year, and the segment isn't expected to return to growth for several quarters.\par \par \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2 's national-security portfolio, which combines its defense and intelligence markets, is in better shape. The company continues to win contracts in these markets, thanks to strong demand for cybersecurity, artificial intelligence and war-fighting technologies, Rozanski said.\par \par \uc2 "The strength in our national-security portfolio cannot offset the current-year decline in our civil business," he said.\par \par \uc2 Booz Allen\uc2 , which makes 98% of its $12 billion in annual revenue from government-related work, now expects adjusted earnings of $5.45 to $5.65 a share for the year. Revenue is projected to fall 4% to 6%, to between $11.3 billion and $11.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The McLean, Va., company had previously guided for adjusted earnings of $6.20 to $6.55 a share, and for revenue to be flat to up 4% this year, to between $12 billion and $12.5 billion. Analysts surveyed by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  are looking for adjusted earnings of $6.30 a share and revenue of $12.06 billion.\par \par \uc2 To offset current headwinds, Calderone said the company would restructure its business to cut $150 million in costs. It would also aim to double down in business lines with growth potential.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251025elap0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
