\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Target to Cut 1,000 Jobs, Eliminate 800 Open Roles --- Retailer's moves would slash 8% of corporate workforce in revamping effort\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Target plans to cut around 1,800 corporate roles as part of an effort to remake its strategy to reverse a period of stagnant sales.\par \par \uc2 Target\uc2  announced Thursday it will lay off around 1,000 global corporate employees and cut 800 open roles. The move will cut around 8% of its approximately 22,000 corporate employees, with around 80% of the cuts in its U.S. ranks.\par \par \uc2 "The truth is, the complexity we've created over time has been holding us back," \uc2 Michael Fiddelke\uc2 , \uc2 Target\uc2 's incoming chief executive, said in a memo sent to staff. "Too many layers and overlapping work have slowed decisions, making it harder to bring ideas to life."\par \par \uc2 The company will share details of the changes Tuesday and asked all U.S. corporate employees to work from home next week, he said in the memo. "It will be difficult," said Fiddelke. "It's a necessary step."\par \par \uc2 The company's stock closed up 0.2% Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Target\uc2  has reported 11 consecutive quarters of falling or weak comparable sales growth and named Fiddelke as its next CEO over the summer.\par \par \uc2 Fiddelke, who has spent his career at Target, will officially take the role in February, but in August said he was already working to speed up the pace of improvements, including assessing the company's headquarters structure, and refreshing the retailer's sharp focus on design.\par \par \uc2 Fiddelke wants to improve customers' shopping experience and speed up the adoption of technology inside the company, but style and design are "our North Star," said in an interview at the company's Minneapolis office in August. "\uc2 Target\uc2  is being its most Target when those things are true," Fiddelke said.\par \par \uc2 The company's stock dropped 6.3% the day Fiddelke was announced as the retailer's next CEO and that longtime CEO \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2  would stay on as executive chairman of \uc2 Target\uc2 's board, as many investors hoped for an outside candidate as change agent.\par \par \uc2 Managers will be affected at a higher rate than individual corporate employees, said a Target spokesman. Affected employees are being paid through Jan. 3, in addition to potential severance payouts, he said.\par \par \uc2 Many Target workers, in a companywide survey in June, said they had grown frustrated with the company's direction. Roughly half of those surveyed said they didn't think the retailer was making the changes necessary to compete effectively, The Wall Street Journal reported. Around 40% of those surveyed said they didn't have confidence in the company's future.\par \par \uc2 Target reports quarterly earnings next month.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251024elao0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
