\par\par \b \uc2 In Supercharged AI World, Workweek Can Top 100 Hours\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Olson and Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 1,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Josh Batson no longer has time for social media.\par \par \uc2 The AI researcher's only comparable dopamine hit these days is on \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's \uc2 Slack\uc2  workplace messaging channels, where he explores chatter about colleagues' theories and experiments on large language models and architecture.\par \par \uc2 Batson is among a group of core AI researchers and executives who are facing a relentless grind, racing to keep pace with a seemingly endless cycle of disruption in pursuit of systems with superhuman intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Inside Silicon Valley's biggest AI labs, top researchers and executives are regularly working 80 to 100 hours a week. Several top researchers compared the circumstances to war.\par \par \uc2 "We're basically trying to speedrun 20 years of scientific progress in two years," said Batson, a research scientist at \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 . Extraordinary advances in AI systems are happening "every few months," he said. "It's the most interesting scientific question in the world right now."\par \par \uc2 Executives and researchers at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  have said they see their work as critical to a seminal moment in history as they duel with rivals and seek new ways to bring AI to the masses.\par \par \uc2 Some of them are now millionaires many times over, but several acknowledged that their lives have scarcely changed. They simply haven't had time to spend their new fortunes.\par \par \uc2 The AI talent wars kicked into high gear when \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  began poaching top AI workers from rivals with multimillion-dollar pay packages. That showed how the work product of a relatively small cluster of researchers and executives was one of the world's most precious resources. Now companies and the workers themselves are seeking to wring as much work as possible from each and every day.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone is working all the time, it's extremely intense and there doesn't seem to be any kind of natural stopping point," Madhavi Sewak, a distinguished researcher at \uc2 Google\uc2 's DeepMind, said in a recent interview.\par \par \uc2 Certain startups have included the expectation of more than 80 hours a week into employment contracts, people familiar with the matter said. But many companies haven't had to, as top AI employees are motivated by the intense competition with rivals and curiosity about new model possibilities.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Meta\uc2 , members of TBD Lab -- the team of new hires tasked with developing the company's AI models -- work in person near Zuckerberg's desk at the company's Menlo Park headquarters.\par \par \uc2 While round-the-clock schedules have been common among startups at various boom times in Silicon Valley's history, extreme hours at some of the world's biggest companies have been far more rare.\par \par \uc2 The most intense periods for many come while working on models or new products, and the work schedule takes up more time than the so-called "9-9-6" schedule, which stands for 9 a.m. to 9 p.m., six days per week. One startup executive jokingly referred to the schedule as "0-0-2," which means midnight to midnight, with a two-hour break on weekends.\par \par \uc2 The breakneck pace is concentrated among a relatively small subset of workers at different companies, many of whom are trying to improve core AI models or take emerging capabilities and embed them into new products. These small teams often work at all hours, even after many lesser-paid colleagues have gone home, workers said.\par \par \uc2 And although many said the grind has worn them down and prevented them from spending time with families and friends, they also said repeatedly that they work long hours by choice.\par \par \uc2 "You have all these good ideas and you know it's a competition against time," Sewak said. "You don't want to let ideas go unexplored, so when you have free time you're working on" and chasing other new ideas.\par \par \uc2 All around the valley, companies have begun to accommodate employees who are in the office almost nonstop. Some serve food on weekends. And some ensure staffing is in place around the clock. Companies appoint "captains" for either short rotations to closely monitor model outputs or extended, multiweek periods where they are overseeing product development, AI workers said.\par \par \uc2 Aparna Chennapragada\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's chief product officer for AI experiences, said she had been through past moments of extreme urgency in tech, but this one is different.\par \par \uc2 In previous boom times, such as the dot-com boom in the late 1990s or the move to mobile after the iPhone, user adoption happened over a decade or more. With AI, 90% of the Fortune 500 are already using AI products after just a few years, she said.\par \par \uc2 The time between research breakthroughs and product launches has compressed from several years in the past to "the gap between Thursday and Friday" in the AI age, creating immense demand that every company is scrambling to meet, she said.\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's Batson said he feels like it is hard to plan or schedule work because of how AI models are advancing and how hard it is to know ahead of time how they will behave. The process more closely resembles evolution than engineering, he said.\par \par \uc2 "You often don't exactly know what you're going to get out of training until you've got it," he said. "And you don't exactly know what it's going to do until you test it. And even then, you don't know exactly what it'll do until it's deployed in the wild."\par \par \uc2 He compared the nonstop nature of the experience to his work in a stand-up testing lab during the Covid-19 pandemic, when he coordinated with a small group of international scientists to understand the trajectory of the virus.\par \par \uc2 His company's stated concern for building ethical, human-aligned AI fuels his work ethic, Batson said. He and co-workers are trying "to see if we can increase our understanding at a faster rate than the models are changing and I think we are hitting that goal."\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's Sewak said that she has enjoyed seeing top AI researchers get paid for all of their smarts and work, even if many continue to be motivated by their work. "I'm very excited that nerds are having our moment."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251023elan0001z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
