\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Tests Robots, AI Tools for Workers --- Retail giant invests in technologies to help it ship packages with less human labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean McLain \par \uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence that makes humans more efficient and robots that make them less necessary: That is the future \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  is building in its e-commerce fulfillment business.\par \par \uc2 The retail giant unveiled a trio of new technologies Wednesday that it is testing or preparing to deploy in its warehouses and delivery vans. They include a robot arm called Blue Jay, designed to sort packages; an artificial-intelligence agent called Eluna, intended to help human managers deploy workers and avoid bottlenecks; and augmented-reality glasses to be worn by delivery drivers in the field.\par \par \uc2 The announcements are the latest in a yearslong effort by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to automate more warehouse tasks, an effort that began with the company's $775 million acquisition of \uc2 Kiva Systems\uc2  in 2012. Around three-quarters of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's deliveries are in some way assisted by robots, the company has said.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  is pushing for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to embrace AI tools even more than it already has. He has said he expected the result to be a diminished need for white-collar workers. Wednesday's announcement shows how that same trend might play out on the warehouse floor. The average number of workers in \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities fell to around 670 in 2024, the lowest in 16 years, according to a Wall Street Journal analysis.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to see billions of dollars in cost savings every year as it automates more of the logistics process, both through increased efficiency and reduced need for humans. The company is projected to have nearly 40 delivery fulfillment centers equipped with robots by the end of next year, which \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  estimated would result in up to $4 billion in cost savings.\par \par \uc2 Blue Jay was developed in a little more than a year, versus three years for previous models, because of the ability to use generative AI to make virtual prototypes, said Tye Brady, chief technologist for \uc2 Amazon Robotics\uc2 , at the company's Delivering the Future conference in San Francisco Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "Many years of hard work are now being supercharged by the latest advances in AI," Brady said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it is trialing the Blue Jay robots at a facility in South Carolina, with the goal of deploying them in a network of same-day delivery centers that will enable cheaper and faster package deliveries.\par \par \uc2 Robots like Blue Jay that can function in tight spaces also allow \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to build new, smaller warehouses in urban areas, where it couldn't operate previously because it couldn't purchase enough warehouse space, Brady said.\par \par \uc2 Eluna is scheduled for a test run at a Tennessee warehouse, where the company says managers will be able to ask it questions such as: "Where should we shift people to avoid a bottleneck?"\par \par \uc2 For the company's fleet of delivery drivers, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  showed off a pair of AI-powered AR glasses that can identify the correct packages to deliver, provide turn-by-turn directions to drop-off locations and even flag the presence of dogs. Many of these functions are currently handled by smartphones carried by the drivers. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it tested early versions of the device with hundreds of drivers, but didn't say if it planned to roll the technology out for broader use.\par \par \uc2 The number of packages each driver carries has nearly doubled over the past five years, managers of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  last-mile delivery fleets say. Delays in locating the correct drop-off location is a common way that drivers fall behind, the managers say.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251023elan0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
