\par\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Cuts 600 Jobs in AI Division --- Layoffs will affect legacy teams but not the company's new TBD Lab unit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  is cutting about 600 jobs in its AI division, according to an internal memo that was viewed by The Wall Street Journal and a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts will affect the company's teams focused on artificial-intelligence products, infrastructure and long-term AI research, but won't touch TBD Lab, the new team that houses most of \uc2 Meta\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 's multimillion-dollar hires, the memo said.\par \par \uc2 "By reducing the size of our team, fewer conversations will be required to make a decision, and each person will be more load-bearing and have more scope and impact," wrote \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief AI officer.\par \par \uc2 He said that there are no changes to the broader Meta Superintelligence Labs structure, which was put together over the summer to help \uc2 Meta\uc2  revamp its AI efforts and that the company plans to continue hiring AI-native talent. \uc2 Meta\uc2  recently poached \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2  co-founder Andrew Tulloch, the Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Most of the affected employees will have the opportunity to be "redeployed" to other jobs at \uc2 Meta\uc2  where their AI expertise could be useful, Wang said. Axios previously reported some details of the job cuts.\par \par \uc2 Since the lackluster launch of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's latest Llama large language model in the spring, the company has been on a crusade to reset its AI plans and stay competitive with other leading companies racing to develop cutting-edge AI technology.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  acquired a 49% stake in startup \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  and hired its CEO, Wang, to run a newly formed AI division focused on developing so-called superintelligence. Zuckerberg also became the company's recruiter-in-chief, personally reaching out to prospective AI hires to invite them to his homes for meals and pitches on why they should join his company. Another part of the pitch was lofty multimillion-dollar pay packages.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  ultimately managed to poach more than 50 engineers, researchers and other employees from competitors like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 . Many of those hires are part of the secretive TBD Lab team at \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which has taken over development of the company's models.\par \par \uc2 That team sits near Zuckerberg's desk at \uc2 Meta\uc2 's Menlo Park, Calif., headquarters and has separate badges to get into their designated section. None of the people on the team were affected by Wednesday's cuts, according to the memo.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which has already increased the high end of its capital-expenditure projections for the year once, reports third-quarter earnings next week. The company previously said it planned to spend up to $72 billion on capex this year, largely on its AI endeavors.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251023elan0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
