\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  to Eliminate 9% of Americas Workforce\b0\par\par\uc2 By Connor Hart \par \uc2 317 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  is eliminating about 400 salaried jobs across its Americas business, which comes as the beer industry contends with a widespread slump.\par \par \uc2 The cuts, expected to be complete by the end of the year, represent about 9% of the salaried workforce in its Americas business, the company said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The number includes hundreds of roles that were already open and others that may be granted voluntary severance as part of the restructuring.\par \par \uc2 The restructuring will streamline operations, allowing the maker of Miller Lite and Blue Moon to focus on growth areas and invest in new product categories, it said.\par \par \uc2 The company aims to return its beer portfolio to growth, while also expanding into adjacent categories including mixers, nonalcohol beverages and energy drinks.\par \par \uc2 "We've made progress on our transformation journey, but given the environment, we must transform even faster," Chief Executive \uc2 Rahul Goyal\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  had expected sentiment to improve throughout the year, but that hasn't been the case. The company lowered its full-year outlook in August, citing weak beer demand, rising aluminum costs and slower-than-expected market share gains.\par \par \uc2 Other beer companies are struggling as well, as more people cut back on alcohol, especially younger consumers who prefer canned cocktails or nonalcoholic beverages. Expanding legalization of cannabis and the use of GLP-1 weight-loss drugs also are eroding sales.\par \par \uc2 Fear and uncertainty driven by the Trump administration's sweeping deportations have prompted the pullback of many Hispanic shoppers, further hurting beer sales, as previously reported by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Molson said it expects to record charges of $35 million to $50 million tied to the restructuring, mostly during the fourth quarter, largely stemming from cash severance payments and postemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Molson Coors\uc2  retreated 1.5% on the New York Stock Exchange in Monday's trading.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251021elal0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
