\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Firm Brings Outsourcing to Rural Areas --- Provalus looks for small towns where incomes are low and people are eager\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Weber \par \uc2 1,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TAHLEQUAH, Okla. -- Hiring is stalling out in much of America, but in small towns like this one, one company is on a recruiting spree.\par \par \uc2 Provalus, which provides big companies with outsourced services such as insurance-claims processing and cybersecurity monitoring, is expanding revenue by 35% to 40% a year with an unusual playbook: investing in rural America. The company has around 250 open jobs now, with hundreds more in the pipeline.\par \par \uc2 Instead of locating its service and technology jobs in a metro area or sending them to countries such as the Philippines, Provalus seeks out small towns, mostly in the South, looking for places where the average individual income hovers around $30,000.\par \par \uc2 There, according to company executives, Provalus finds people who are eager for jobs that will teach them 21st-century skills but who have few opportunities. It is a stab at reversing decades of disinvestment in rural communities, and a model for creating jobs in often-overlooked pockets of the country.\par \par \uc2 "There is an untapped labor market in rural America," said Chuck Ruggiero, an outsourcing executive who founded Provalus in 2017, years after the 9/11 attacks prompted him to look for ways to create jobs in the U.S. "They have the grit, the grind, and many have the aptitude."\par \par \uc2 To identify that aptitude, Provalus created assessments that help it find the food-service or retail workers who might have a bright future in tech or management roles.\par \par \uc2 "I came from a restaurant background with no experience," said Carole Kemison, who, along with her husband, was hired and trained by Provalus. She was hired in 2024 and recently was promoted to a $17-an-hour role managing workers who monitor fraud concerns for a bank client.\par \par \uc2 No major highways or railroads run through Tahlequah. The town of 16,000 in northeastern Oklahoma has a poverty rate of 20.3% -- about 8 percentage points higher than the national average.\par \par \uc2 It is perhaps best known as the capital of the \uc2 Cherokee Nation\uc2  and the setting for the 1961 novel "Where the Red Fern Grows," about a boy who adopts two dogs and trains them to hunt.\par \par \uc2 Paradoxically, those qualities made it an attractive place for Provalus to open its fourth location in 2022. The company has three more offices opening in other towns in 2026, bringing the total to 12. In Tahlequah, it has 200 employees and is finishing a second facility that will hold 100 more. Training classes with up to 40 people are almost always in session as new employees come aboard.\par \par \uc2 Around 50 million people, or 1 in 7 Americans, live in rural areas. These regions generate less than 10% of gross domestic product, most of which comes from resource-intensive industries such as agriculture, mining and manufacturing, according to McKinsey.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a degree of retreat from investing in rural Oklahoma," said Chuck Hoskin Jr., principal chief of the \uc2 Cherokee Nation\uc2 , which purchased a building for Provalus to lease. Politicians, he said, "love small-town America and countrysides, but not very many put their money where their mouth is."\par \par \uc2 So the arrival of IT and professional-service jobs is something of a novelty in Tahlequah, as well as Monticello, Ky., another community where Provalus operates, and Brewton, Ala., the company's headquarters town.\par \par \uc2 The company is a unit of OPS, a services company that includes Optomi, a staffing firm that supplies highly skilled technology contractors. OPS is partly owned by \uc2 FFL Partners\uc2 , a San Francisco-based private-equity firm.\par \par \uc2 Companies including \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 , \uc2 Navy Federal Credit Union\uc2  and the internet provider Brightspeed outsource a small portion of their IT or back-office work to Provalus. Drawn by the company's mission, they also reap the benefits of onshore services: Time zones line up, data is easier to protect and site visits don't require overnight flights.\par \par \uc2 Those advantages, along with what executives say is higher productivity and quality, allow Provalus to compete with big offshore competitors such as \uc2 Infosys\uc2 , \uc2 Cognizant Technology Solutions\uc2  and \uc2 Wipro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With around 1,200 employees, Provalus is dwarfed by those outsourcing giants, which each employ hundreds of thousands of workers, mostly in India but also the Philippines, Vietnam, Mexico and other lower-cost countries. Their services can cost 20% to 30% less than Provalus.\par \par \uc2 The simple reason, of course, is wages. That makes places such as Tahlequah appealing to Provalus and its customers. In small towns, Provalus competes for workers with employers including \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , so entry-level pay starts low, at roughly $13 or $14 an hour for workers who might have college degrees or years of work experience in other fields.\par \par \uc2 Unlike with most hourly jobs, the wage potential at Provalus is high for some, and the benefits package is generous. Health-insurance costs $1 per paycheck. For the first six months, employees receive scheduled raises every 60 days, and another at the one-year mark. After that, pay increases are merit-based.\par \par \uc2 "That allows us to create middle-class jobs quickly," said Mike Keogh, Provalus's president.\par \par \uc2 When Provalus opened in Tahlequah, it told the town that salaries there would average around $38,000, or 27% more than the local average income of around $30,000. A handful of nonexecutive employees at the Tahlequah office earn or will soon earn six figures.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Kimble is on that path. He worked full time managing a local \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's while finishing his computer-science degree at \uc2 Northeastern State University\uc2  and was hired by Provalus in 2022.\par \par \uc2 "There wasn't a lot in the 30-mile area, and I wasn't too keen on moving around," said Kimble, 25 years old, who grew up in Stilwell, Okla. "Family is important to me. It kind of limited my scope of work."\par \par \uc2 Kimble's start was rocky, he said, but eventually he landed on a cybersecurity team. A corporate client noticed his work and asked him to become a security engineer on its account. His pay has nearly tripled since he joined the company.\par \par \uc2 The jobs aren't for everyone. Turnover averages 30% to 40% a year, Keogh said. When people leave, they often say the work -- such as phone calls with people confused about their insurance benefits or whose laptops have stopped working -- was too hard.\par \par \uc2 But the roles have proved to be a good source of work for people who often have complicated lives -- single parents needing some flexibility, soldiers who are transitioning out of the military, middle-aged workers whose bodies can no longer handle physically demanding blue-collar jobs. Around 31% of the company's employees have an associate or bachelor's degree.\par \par \uc2 For employees who might experience a bout of homelessness, most Provalus offices have showers and laundry rooms. The company has occasionally put workers up in hotels while connecting them with public housing. There are "community closets" in every location to help employees put together a professional wardrobe.\par \par \uc2 The opportunities might not satisfy everyone's ambitions.\par \par \uc2 Ubaid Khan, a junior computer-science major at \uc2 Northeastern State University\uc2 , arrived at a job fair on the Tahlequah campus earlier this month in a blue suit, ready to make a good impression. He hadn't heard of Provalus before he arrived but stopped by the company's booth.\par \par \uc2 He might apply for an internship, he said, but his real goal is to move west and get a six-figure job at a startup.\par \par \uc2 "Hopefully, I'll get a chance to work for Provalus," he said. "Then I'll go to San Francisco and achieve my dream."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251020elak0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
