\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- A Decade-Old Flop Is Now Held Up As \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's Shining Success --- The largest private employer in the U.S., long a poster child for low pay, gave its workers a big raise. Back then, investors thought it was a bad move.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 1,401 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bentonville, Ark. -- A decade ago, the largest private employer in the U.S., \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , increased its starting wage to $9 an hour. Raising the salaries of nearly half of its more than a million U.S. hourly workers made it the biggest pay raise in history. Investors reacted by sending \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shares down 10%, destroying $21.5 billion in market value in hours.\par \par \uc2 This fall, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's experience will be published as a \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  case study -- as a success. Hundreds of executives from Blackstone, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and other firms traveled to Bentonville recently to learn about \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's future workforce management and hear the story of the 2015 wage increase from \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's chief executive. The move, he says, launched its current sales tear and online advance.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Walmart\uc2  decided to raise pay for the hourly workers in its stores and warehouses, who now number 1.5 million, it wasn't trying to be benevolent. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  was in the crosshairs of labor activists. Turnover was high, many workers were miserable and shopping at \uc2 Walmart\uc2  was often a bad customer experience.\par \par \uc2 On top of all that, the world's largest retailer was facing a dire business situation. It had grown so big that its own executives doubted that it could keep growing. It also faced a rival who wouldn't stop growing, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Because supercenters blanketed much of America, many \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives had been asking, "Are we too big to grow?," said Chief Executive \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2  in an interview, looking back on discussions he had before taking the top job in 2014. "Some people developed a feeling that it was just too hard," he said. As a result, the company had been focusing on improving profits by keeping down costs -- including wages -- while sales stagnated.\par \par \uc2 But as McMillon started his new job and talked to workers he heard that they needed higher wages, steady schedules, less inventory clutter in backrooms, consistent low prices and more middle managers in the stores, he said.\par \par \uc2 He crafted a plan to raise wages. He brought in a new crop of \uc2 Walmart\uc2  leaders, including new U.S. chief executive and chief operating officers. Their proposal would reduce employee turnover, which would improve operations in the stores and warehouses. They could invest in more training to get workers to stick around with promotions. Stores would be more organized. Sales would increase, and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  would be better positioned for e-commerce, the argument went. The board approved -- and said they needed to move faster than proposed to boost pay.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 Walmart\uc2  announced in February 2015 that it would raise wages above the then-federal minimum wage of $7.25 an hour, other retailers quickly followed including Target and \uc2 TJX\uc2 , owner of TJ Maxx. A week later, President Obama called to congratulate McMillon from Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 That fall, at an investor meeting at the New York Stock Exchange, then-Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Charles Holley\uc2  clicked through his slide presentation for analysts and quantified the change. It would be a $2.7 billion cost over two years, plus more later to lower prices, improve stores and grow online. Earnings per share would drop by 6% to 12% the following year due to the investments. As the slides lingered on the screen, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shares began to tumble.\par \par \uc2 "You clean up your house before you invite people over," McMillon told the room to explain that he planned to focus on improving the efficiency and appearance of \uc2 Walmart\uc2  stores, and then on lowering prices to attract more shoppers.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shares continued their free fall, and executives made an unplanned exit from the stage to regroup before returning to take questions.\par \par \uc2 As they headed to the airport to take the corporate plane back to Bentonville, CNBC host \uc2 Jim Cramer\uc2  called, angry that McMillon hadn't shared the news earlier in the day during a more routine investor day interview. They turned the car around to do a last-minute segment on CNBC "to try and put a floor on what was happening with the share price that day," said McMillon.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shares have more than doubled over the past five years. Its U.S. sales have grown each year since 2015 and global sales hit $681 billion last year, helping it maintain its spot as the country's largest retailer by revenue.\par \par \uc2 Hubert Joly\uc2 , a senior lecturer at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  and a former chief executive of electronics retailer \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , studies the role of front-line workers in business success. He liked that the market initially hated a plan that would turn into the foundation for \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's biggest strategy shift and recent success.\par \par \uc2 Last summer he approached \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives to see if they would talk to him for a \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  case study, and they agreed.\par \par \uc2 Joly hopes students use the case he authored with two other Harvard faculty to better understand the return on investments in workers. "My goal is to encourage more business leaders to have the courage, but also the tools, to be able to make that decision," he said.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  hosted a workforce-planning conference in its shiny new Bentonville headquarters for hundreds of human-resource executives from across the country. Joly and McMillon presented the Harvard findings.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  didn't make a big jump in pay all at once and it didn't try to be a leader. It moved from the bottom rung of hourly pay to just above the middle compared with competitors. Average hourly pay for U.S. Walmart workers has risen from around $12 an hour in 2015 to over $18.25 an hour as of July for its 1.5 million U.S. hourly employees. It has also added other benefits such as parental leave, more job training and free college and technical education. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has improved its hourly staff-retention rate by over 10% since 2015, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Stores needed stability and better-organized inventory to work as e-commerce hubs, said \uc2 Greg Foran\uc2 , CEO of Walmart U.S. from 2014 to 2019. "We were paying one of the lowest rates in retail and while that wasn't the only reason for significant turnover, it was a big one," he said.\par \par \uc2 Before 2015 \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives were tasked with a different mission, said \uc2 Bill Simon\uc2 , Walmart U.S. chief executive from 2010 to 2014. They were asked to deliver cash to grow digitally to compete with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  while keeping prices low, he said in an interview. "Every meeting I was ever in was about how do you provide more to customers for less."\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  kept raising wages, but not as fast as some competitors. In 2018 \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said it would pay workers at least $11 per hour and would offer full-time store workers the same paid family leave as corporate employees. By the following year \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Costco\uc2  said their minimum wage would be $15. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  built out a network of hundreds of regional academies that prepare workers for promotions.\par \par \uc2 Rissa Pittman, then a store manager in Ponca City, Okla., said it was easier to staff her store after 2015 as wages improved and it became easier to train workers for promotions. "I could really emphasize that there's more to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 ," said Pittman, who started as a cashier in 2003 and is now vice president of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's Pacific Northwest region overseeing 30,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 Today, the company is adding automation to its warehouses that will cut the number of workers inside, and executives say artificial intelligence will remake its labor force in the coming years. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  expects its 2.1 million global workforce will stay steady over the next three years even as revenue grows. To manage costs, it's also shrunk starting wages so more people come in at $14.\par \par \uc2 Perks such as family leave benefits, bonuses and a clear route to a promotion keep workers engaged and around long term, the company's ultimate goal, said McMillon. "Hourly wage matters, and we want to be a little north of average on that number," he said. "It's a system. It's not one metric."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251018elai0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
