\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobless Claims By Federal Workers Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 350 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The number of unemployment claims filed by federal workers has grown sharply, reaching their highest level since a 34-day government shutdown that ended in January 2019.\par \par \uc2 Data collected by state unemployment insurance program offices show that last week 7,244 federal workers filed an initial unemployment claim. That was up from 3,272 the previous week, and 588 in the last full week before the current government shutdown, which started Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 The number of federal workers who had already filed for unemployment, and were continuing to claim benefits, rose to 9,430 in the week ended Oct. 4 from 8,672 the previous week.\par \par \uc2 The jump in claims likely reflects two forces. The shutdown has prompted many temporarily furloughed workers to file for unemployment. And some of the workers who took the Trump administration's deferred-resignation plan -- and recently lost their paychecks -- might be filing for unemployment.\par \par \uc2 The claims figures provide a glimpse into the roiling of the federal workforce at a time when most official economic reports have been postponed by the shutdown. Although the Labor Department hasn't been releasing the weekly jobless claims figures it usually reports each Thursday, the state data that informs it are available.\par \par \uc2 Following the 2018-2019 shutdown, furloughed workers received retroactive pay, requiring them to return any unemployment benefits they received while the government was closed. Whether that happens after the current shutdown ends is less clear; the administration has challenged the 2019 law that guarantees automatic back pay for all federal employees once the shutdown concludes.\par \par \uc2 Workers struggling to keep up with bills while they go without pay are likely filing for unemployment benefits even if they think they will eventually get back pay, according to Oxford Economics lead U.S. economist \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 . And the longer the shutdown persists, the higher the number of claims will probably go. The two-week government pay cycle that ended Friday will be the first one for which the furloughed workers don't get even a partial paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251018elai0002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
