\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: 'Stop the Steal' Lawyer Hired for 2020 Investigation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josh Dawsey, Eliza Collins and C. Ryan Barber \par \uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A former Trump campaign lawyer who worked on the effort to overturn the 2020 election results has joined the administration to investigate that year's election and voting-related issues, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Kurt Olsen, a lawyer who unsuccessfully pushed claims of voter fraud, has joined the administration as a "special government employee," some of the people said. The appointment gives Olsen 130 days to work from within the White House without giving up any private business interests. He is talking directly with President Trump, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Trump asked Olsen to join the administration to work on election issues important to him, a senior administration official said. Olsen started his job in the past month, some of the people said, and has told others he wants to examine election machines.\par \par \uc2 Olsen has begun asking intelligence agencies for information about the 2020 election, the officials said. Olsen has told others he also wants to weed out some government employees who he believes are disloyal to Trump.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for the White House and Justice Department declined to comment, and Olsen didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Trump lost the 2020 election and tried to overturn the results by alleging voter fraud across several states. He was helped in his effort, dubbed "Stop the Steal," by lawyers who filed related lawsuits. Olsen in particular sought to appeal the loss to the Supreme Court on behalf of Texas.\par \par \uc2 The broader effort culminated in the riot at the U.S. Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021, and Trump's subsequent impeachment. Since returning to office, Trump has pardoned nearly all of the more than 1,500 people charged in connection with the attack, while commuting the sentences of others, and installed some of their advocates in administration jobs. Trump and his allies are also revisiting the special counsel probe that resulted in criminal charges alleging he plotted to overturn the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 "He has effectively changed the factual history of that event in a lot of people's minds already," said \uc2 Marc Short\uc2 , who was chief of staff to Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  as Trump and Olsen tried to overturn the results, and who has been critical of Trump.\par \par \uc2 As recently as this past week, Trump said the special counsel who investigated him, Jack Smith, was himself a criminal and pointed to him as a target for prosecution. And at a Senate hearing this month, Republican lawmakers repeatedly denounced Smith and the Biden-era \uc2 FBI\uc2  for collecting the phone records of more than a half-dozen senators as part of that investigation, in an effort to determine with whom they had spoken in the days surrounding Jan. 6.\par \par \uc2 On social media, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel said he had fired "those who acted unethically" and opened an investigation into Smith's subpoenas of the senators' phone records.\par \par \uc2 In late 2020, Olsen contacted senior Justice Department officials on Trump's behalf and pushed them to file a motion to nullify the election with the Supreme Court, according to documents obtained by the House panel that investigated the Jan. 6 attack. The draft complaint urged the high court to order a special election for president in some of the states where Trump lost, according to the committee. The acting attorney general at the time, Jeffrey Rosen, ignored the requests. Trump talked to Olsen several times on Jan. 6, the committee found.\par \par \uc2 Olsen's appointment comes as the Justice Department has sought voter information from states including those that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  narrowly won in 2020. Many Democratic secretaries of states have resisted the efforts, saying they break privacy laws and are an overreach of power.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251018elai00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
