\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Judge Temporarily Bars Move to Fire Federal Employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lydia Wheeler and Ken Thomas \par \uc2 481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge temporarily blocked the Trump administration from moving ahead with mass firings of federal employees while the government is shut down.\par \par \uc2 Judge Susan Illston issued the temporary restraining order in a ruling from the bench Wednesday, stopping the government from cutting federal workers at multiple agencies.\par \par \uc2 The court record suggested the Trump administration has "taken advantage of the lapse in government spending and government functioning to assume that all bets are off, that the laws don't apply to them anymore and that they can impose the structures that they like on the government situation that they don't like," said Illston, a \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  appointee.\par \par \uc2 The administration moved ahead on threats last week to lay off federal workers, sending reductions in force notices, otherwise known as RIFs, to about 4,000 employees at more than a half-dozen federal agencies, including the departments of Treasury, Health and Human Services, Education and Commerce.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has referred to the cuts as "Democrat-oriented" and has been seeking to increase pressure on Democrats to end the standoff in Congress over government funding.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions representing federal workers sued after the administration first threatened the firings. The unions argue it is unlawful for the \uc2 White House Office of Management and Budget\uc2  to have employees work to carry out RIFs during a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 "Ordering any employee to work during a shutdown to administer a RIF process would directly violate the Antideficiency Act," said attorneys for the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2  and the \uc2 American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees\uc2 . The Antideficiency Act requires agencies to cease most operations during a government shutdown, with only a few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 The unions argue the Trump administration doesn't have the authority to issue these RIFs, is acting unlawfully and failed to give a valid reason for the firings.\par \par \uc2 After OMB Director \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2  announced in an Oct. 10 social-media post that the RIFs had begun, the labor unions asked Illston to immediately stop the administration from implementing any notices during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The administration urged Illston to deny the request. A Justice Department attorney, Elizabeth Hedges, said the majority of the agencies haven't yet decided to send out RIFs. "There's even more decision-making that still has to occur and in many cases there may never be RIFs at all," she said. Illston pressed Hedges repeatedly to detail the legal justification for the RIFs during Wednesday's hearing.\par \par \uc2 Illston said she believes the unions are likely to demonstrate that what the Trump administration is doing has exceeded its legal authority.\par \par \uc2 Illston's order applies to RIFs the government already announced and any it planned to send in the future related to the shutdown. The Trump administration didn't immediately comment on the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251016elag0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
