\par\par \b \uc2 Ford Cuts Truck, SUV Production --- Moneymakers hit after fire knocks key aluminum supplier offline until next year\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts and Bob Tita \par \uc2 787 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  is temporarily cutting production of at least five models including popular SUVs after a devastating fire at a crucial aluminum supplier's plant.\par \par \uc2 Starting this week, Ford isn't producing its lucrative three-row SUVs, the Expedition and Lincoln Navigator, at its Kentucky Truck Plant because of the difficulties with aluminum supply, an official from the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  told members at the plant last week. The SUV pause is set to last a week, according to a memo the company sent to workers and viewed by The Wall Street Journal. The memo didn't specify the reason for the move.\par \par \uc2 Ford declined to provide specifics on any production adjustments. The company said last week that it was working closely with \uc2 Novelis\uc2 , the aluminum supplier whose plant in upstate New York burned last month, while also exploring "all possible alternatives to minimize any potential disruptions."\par \par \uc2 Automakers often schedule downtime throughout the year. In this case, Ford has pulled forward time off slated for later in 2025, according to a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The three-alarm fire knocked the \uc2 Novelis\uc2  plant offline until early next year. The plant supplies about 40% of the aluminum sheet used by the auto industry in the U.S., according to industry analysts. Ford is the plant's biggest customer. Its F-150 pickup, the top-selling truck in the U.S. and the automaker's main profit driver, is one of the industry's biggest users of aluminum.\par \par \uc2 After the fire, analysts warned that Ford was at risk of losing as much as $1 billion of operating profit if production of F-series pickups was lost. So far, Ford is able to keep making those trucks -- including its highly profitable F-series Super Duty pickups -- at the plant in Kentucky. "They're focusing all their energy on making sure all their F-150s get built," said \uc2 Sam Fiorani\uc2 , an analyst with consulting firm AutoForecast Solutions.\par \par \uc2 But a \uc2 UAW\uc2  official told members in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post Monday that the Kentucky plant producing the Super Duty pickups "may run short today, tonight and possibly over the next few shifts."\par \par \uc2 Ford is already facing $2 billion in costs related to President Trump's tariff policies and $5 billion in losses by its electric-vehicle unit this year.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Novelis\uc2  plant in upstate New York aims to resume production of automotive-grade aluminum sheet early next year, some analysts project it will take until March or April. Meanwhile, the company is looking to import aluminum from other countries.\par \par \uc2 Ford's stock is up 18% so far this year. Despite headwinds, the automaker is benefiting from loosened fuel-economy regulations that allow it to focus on large trucks over money-losing EVs.\par \par \uc2 To preserve aluminum supply, Ford also stopped work at its other assembly plant in Louisville, Ky., last week, according to a \uc2 UAW\uc2  official, resuming this week with only one of two shifts operating.\par \par \uc2 That plant is in the final months of producing the Escape SUV and its luxury cousin, the Lincoln Corsair. The Escape will end production in December, the official said, as Ford prepares to build a new electric pickup at the Louisville plant.\par \par \uc2 Ford this week is also idling its Dearborn, Mich., plant that produces its current electric pickup, the F-150 Lightning, because of the aluminum issue, Reuters reported last week.\par \par \uc2 Atlanta-based \uc2 Novelis\uc2  said it was working to determine whether automotive aluminum from its overseas plants and its competitors' plants can meet Ford's standards. The qualification process for an automaker typically takes several months. But because \uc2 Novelis\uc2  is already supplying automakers in other countries and its competitors are producing automotive aluminum as well, \uc2 Novelis\uc2  expects the qualification process to go faster for automakers that were supplied by the New York plant.\par \par \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , another \uc2 Novelis\uc2  customer, has been working on getting aluminum from alternative sources for its North American plants, a spokesman said. The automaker hasn't had any production disruptions but is "not out of the woods quite yet," he added.\par \par \uc2 Ford switched the F-series pickups' exteriors to aluminum from steel a decade ago to make the trucks lighter and improve fuel economy.\par \par \uc2 F-series trucks are vital to Ford's profitability. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  analysts said that a 20% reduction in F-series sales in the fourth quarter could cut Ford's operating income by $800 million. Analysts at Evercore ISI estimated that about 20,000 to 50,000 F-series trucks could be lost, of about 130,000 expected to be assembled in the final two months of the year.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251015elaf0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
