\par\par \b \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to Invest $13 Billion in U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton \par \uc2 324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plans to spend billions of dollars to make more Jeeps, pickups and Dodge SUVs in the U.S., in what the automaker describes as the largest single investment in its history.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said Tuesday that it would spend $13 billion through the end of the decade as it launches five new vehicles and a new four-cylinder engine, creating more than 5,000 jobs at plants across the Midwest. Suppliers providing parts for those models may add 20,000 jobs, Chief Executive \uc2 Antonio Filosa\uc2  said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal. "The most important and relevant objective that we have with this investment is to grow in this market," Filosa said.\par \par \uc2 The plan could help one of the country's largest car manufacturers defray costs from tariffs imposed by President Trump. The company expects a total tariff impact of $1.5 billion for the year.\par \par \uc2 The automaker relies heavily on North America, which historically has driven a majority of profits.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said that vehicle shipments in North America rose 35% in the most recent quarter, compared with a year earlier, as the company started deliveries of Ram 1500 pickups with Hemi V-8 engines.\par \par \uc2 Under Filosa, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  had been working through a plan to reduce excess unsold supply in the U.S., a byproduct of earlier stumbles under prior Chief Executive \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2 . \uc2 Jeep\uc2  saw sales fall off dramatically. The company's board ousted Tavares in late 2024.\par \par \uc2 The investment plans include a mix of gasoline-powered vehicles and electrified models at plants in Michigan, Indiana, Illinois, and Ohio. In the interview, Filosa said additional plans will be announced in the future for the company's other key American brands, including Chrysler and Dodge.\par \par \uc2 A midsize pickup the company planned to build at an idled factory in Belvidere, Ill., will be built at its assembly complex in Toledo, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251015elaf0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
