\par\par \b \uc2 Shutdown Is Finally Starting to Bite\b0\par\par\uc2 By Siobhan Hughes, Theo Francis and Katy Stech Ferek \par \uc2 1,054 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The government shutdown is starting to get real.\par \par \uc2 The Smithsonian museums down the hill from the U.S. Capitol are now closed. Federal workers are already starting to miss pay and will have to rely on savings until the government reopens. Some businesses that count on a fully functioning federal government say layoffs and costly delays will accelerate.\par \par \uc2 President Trump over the weekend removed one of the most potent consequences of the shutdown when he announced that he found a way -- for now -- to pay the 1.3 million active-duty servicemen and women, in addition to hundreds of thousands of members of the military reserves and National Guard, whose payday is Oct. 15.\par \par \uc2 But the strain is deepening for contractors, such as the housekeepers and cement operators, who perform work for the government but aren't guaranteed back wages; airports that rely on federal staff working without pay to keep travel safe; executives planning without government economic data; entrepreneurs unable to secure small-business loans; and consumers unable to close on mortgages without federal flood insurance.\par \par \uc2 "Families around the country are already seeing the impacts, and it's about to get a whole lot worse," said Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  (R., S.D.) at a press conference headed into the Columbus Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Thune and other Republicans blame Senate Democrats for failing to provide the votes to approve a House-passed spending bill to reopen the government through Nov. 21. Democrats want assurances that Congress will extend tens or hundreds of billions of dollars in healthcare funds before they will vote to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 Informal talks are taking place, focused on short-term extensions mixed with changes to the program, but party leaders have remained firmly dug in to their positions.\par \par \uc2 "We need a real negotiation and we need a fix," Sen. Mark Kelly (D., Ariz.) said Sunday on NBC's "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 At a recent town hall in Virginia's northern suburbs, Jewelyn Cosgrove told Rep. Suhas Subramanyam (D., Va.) how the shutdown threatened pay for some of the 1,000 workers at her employer, Melwood, a federal contractor that manages the grounds at the Kennedy Center, J. Edgar Hoover Building and elsewhere. Most of the company's workforce has significant disabilities, such as blindness and limited mobility.\par \par \uc2 "My employees just want to work," Cosgrove said in an interview. "They just want to be able to put their boots on the ground."\par \par \uc2 In southern Nevada, about 150 workers at locations including the Nevada Test Site, which supports national-security missions, had been furloughed as of Friday, according to union leader Tommy Blitsch, who has recommended that the workers file for unemployment insurance.\par \par \uc2 If the shutdown is prolonged, as many as 900 defense contractors from just his union could be furloughed, he estimates.\par \par \uc2 "People don't need to be starving. People don't need to be worried about their kids' health insurance," Blitsch said. He said that lawmakers should take the same advice they gave him when his union threatened a strike: "get in a room, get it done and put people back to work."\par \par \uc2 Party leaders showed no change in their stances. "They've made the decision to take a 'my way or the highway' approach," said House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D., N.Y.) of Republicans, speaking on "Fox News Sunday." House Speaker Mike Johnson (R., La.) countered on the same show that it was Democrats who were being unreasonable. "It's real pain for real people, and they seem not to care," he said.\par \par \uc2 Trump's move to pay troops is coming from $8 billion in unspent \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  funds for research and development, according to the \uc2 White House Office of Management and Budget\uc2 . The military pay followed patches to keep money flowing to a program aimed at women, infants and children and a program funding air travel along routes from rural areas that aren't profitable.\par \par \uc2 Such efforts help families now, but might lessen pressure for Congress to act quickly.\par \par \uc2 The White House "is removing action-forcing catalysts," wrote \uc2 Chris Krueger\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The shutdown is adding to the hassle of daily transactions. Federal loans to small businesses are on hold. Some home sales can't close because the federal government isn't issuing the flood-insurance policies typically required for properties in areas designated by federal flood maps, said Brian Gardner, chief Washington policy strategist for \uc2 Stifel Financial\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With much of staff from the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  on furlough, some companies are struggling to issue stock and other securities.\par \par \uc2 Airport delays are mounting as workers including air-traffic controllers aren't getting paid, an issue that helped force the resolution of the monthlong shutdown that ended in early 2019.\par \par \uc2 Other unpaid workers also tend to miss more work, which could lead to bottlenecks even in functions of the government that have stayed open, such as customs inspectors at ports.\par \par \uc2 "People are hunkering down," said Rep. Dave Min (D., Calif.), who said he has met with air-traffic controllers who showed up to work knowing that they will not get paychecks until after the funding impasse is over. "I know they're struggling to pay the bills, and right now, I think they're OK for a few weeks, but the longer this drags out, the harder it'll be."\par \par \uc2 Corporate executives are most concerned about the lack of government economic data, including on jobs, as well as abrupt shifts in government policy, said \uc2 Greg Daco\uc2 , chief economist for \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon. As a result, they're being more cautious about decisions to invest in expansion and innovation.\par \par \uc2 While the Washington area is home to the most federal workers, thousands more work around the country. The areas around Kansas City, Memphis, Tenn., and Ogden, Utah, have relatively high concentrations of federal employees in their workforce.\par \par \uc2 A prolonged shutdown likely would hit consumer spending, the engine of the U.S. economy, said \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , lead U.S. economist for Oxford Economics. People tend to pull back on spending rapidly when they expect their income to fall even temporarily, and that could be exacerbated given White House warnings that furloughed workers might not receive back pay, she said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251013elad0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
