\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- How Much Pay Will Workers Sacrifice to Work Remotely? A study of tech workers offers an answer: quite a lot\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsey Choo \par \uc2 435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As more tech companies tighten their return-to-office policies, a new study suggests that many job applicants are willing to accept less pay for positions that are either fully remote or on a hybrid schedule.\par \par \uc2 The new study, by researchers at Harvard and Brown universities and UCLA, surveyed nearly 1,400 workers -- a majority of whom were software engineers, product managers and data scientists -- who had at least two job offers and accepted one between May 2023 and December 2024. Those who accepted remote or hybrid work on average agreed to accept 25% less than what they were offered for a similar in-office job that they rejected.\par \par \uc2 So, if a candidate received a job offer of $200,000 that required five days in the office and another that offered $150,000 and allowed working remotely, on average the candidate who wanted to work from home was willing to take a $50,000 pay cut, says co-author Ricardo Perez-Truglia, an economics professor at the UCLA Anderson School of Management. As a control, the offers in the study were compared within groups with the same job title, the same location, the same experience level and type of company.\par \par \uc2 The average amount tech workers were willing to give up was more than the researchers expected, says Perez-Truglia. Several previous studies, conducted between 2017 and 2023, estimated that workers were willing to take pay cuts of 5% to 10% for remote work or a hybrid schedule. One reason for this increase could be that more remote-work jobs exist now, thus workers have more choice, says Perez-Truglia. Also, the salary data in the recent study reflected real offers, instead of hypothetical choices posed in the earlier studies.\par \par \uc2 The study found that subjects rated fully remote work as only slightly more attractive than hybrid jobs that require a few days in the office. And while workers seemed to feel strongly against a five-day policy, they didn't feel the same way about a three-day policy.\par \par \uc2 For companies that want to align their return-to-office policies with what their employees value, "clearly there can be a compromise," Perez-Truglia says. Allowing for just one or two days a week of remote work can make a difference as to whether a person takes a job, he says.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Choo is a writer in New York. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251013elad00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
