\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- Giving Support During Menopause Is the Latest Perk in the Workplace: As employees become more open about symptoms, employers are offering benefits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tara Weiss \par \uc2 1,225 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This past summer, a meme called "Cougar Puberty" made its way into a \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel at public-relations and marketing firm Bospar. The way Denyse Dabrowski remembers it, what began as a joke got women in the office talking about a topic they hadn't discussed before: menopause.\par \par \uc2 "One minute we were laughing and before we knew it we were having a refreshingly honest conversation about brain fog, hormone changes and hot flashes," says the 48-year-old Dabrowski, Bospar's head of strategy and creative. "Women on our team were experiencing very real symptoms that impacted their work, but didn't always have the language or support to name it -- or feel comfortable doing so."\par \par \uc2 As a result of that conversation, Bospar in August launched a comprehensive menopause program that, among other things, offers employees specialized care and flexible work hours to help them manage symptoms like fatigue. The company also encouraged employees to start a menopause-focused resource group, which now has a dozen members and a dedicated \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel called Bospar-HotSpot.\par \par \uc2 As women in the workforce, especially those in leadership positions, become more comfortable talking about how menopause-related symptoms such as insomnia, hot flashes, brain fog, heart palpitations and anxiety affect them on the job, more employers are offering support. Those benefits may include access to menopause specialists, coverage for medical treatments, educational resources and in-office amenities including quiet rooms and "comfort closets" stocked with supplies such as fans and deodorant.\par \par \uc2 The share of U.S. employers with 200 or more employees that offer menopause benefits jumped to 25% this year from 15% in 2023, according to HR consulting firm \uc2 Mercer\uc2 . The goal, many companies say, is to prevent women from dialing back -- or dropping out of the workforce -- at a critical time in their careers.\par \par \uc2 "Menopause affects well-being, productivity and retention," which is why it needs to be addressed, says Kathryn Larkin, the head of global benefits at financial-services firm Vanguard Group, which offers menopause-related benefits such as webinars, workshops and medical coverage for treatments.\par \par \uc2 Women are said to be in menopause after going a year without a period, which typically occurs between the ages of 45 and 55, though it can vary. Hormonal changes can wreak havoc in the years leading up to that point. This transition period, known as perimenopause, lasts seven to nine years on average, often coinciding with women reaching the highest ranks of their careers.\par \par \uc2 Menopause talk has gained momentum in recent years, thanks in part to celebrities such as Naomi Watts, Halle Berry, Gwyneth Paltrow and Drew Barrymore sharing their journeys and treatments, and doctors like Mary Claire Haver, author of "The New Menopause," sharing bite-size videos about menopause on social media. The conversation made its way into the workplace during the pandemic, when formerly taboo topics such as mental health, work-life balance and yes, menopause, became fair game for workplace fodder.\par \par \uc2 The move by employers to offer menopause support also coincides with growing interest in wellness at work, particularly around women and families. Many companies have expanded coverage for fertility treatments, pre- and postnatal care, parental leave and ramped-up reboarding for new mothers returning to work. Menopause is the natural next step in benefits as those women age, says Lindsay Bower, a senior health-benefits consultant at \uc2 Mercer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Women age 50 and older are among the fastest-growing demographics in the workplace, according to the Labor Department. In 2023, a \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  study estimated the cost of missed work due to menopause-related symptoms is $1.8 billion annually in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "That is not a number that gets ignored," says Bower.\par \par \uc2 The issue has even caught the attention of some state lawmakers. In June, Rhode Island became the first state to mandate "reasonable" workplace accommodations for employees experiencing menopause-related symptoms, such as access to temperature-controlled workstations and flexibility for time off for doctor's appointments.\par \par \uc2 The bill was introduced by State Sen. Lori Urso, who says she herself experienced menopause-related insomnia so severe from 2017 to 2020 that she considered stepping away from her other job as president and CEO of Slater Industrial Archives in Pawtucket, R.I.\par \par \uc2 "You lose confidence at work and retreat from the leadership pipeline," says Urso, age 61. "That's why this is a workplace issue now."\par \par \uc2 Women say that unlike menstruation and pregnancy, guidance from doctors about menopause can be hard to come by, so they often struggle to find the source of their ailments.\par \par \uc2 The lack of knowledge is largely because medical professionals weren't trained on symptom management following a 2002 study by the Women's Health Initiative that suggested hormone replacement therapy causes breast cancer, strokes and blood clot, says Stephanie Faubion, chair of the department of medicine at the Mayo Clinic Center for Women's Health and medical director of the Menopause Society.\par \par \uc2 That study has since been updated to show that hormone therapy is a safe and effective treatment for many woman in perimenopause, she says. Still, misperceptions about menopause and available therapies linger.\par \par \uc2 To help compensate for that, some employers are providing access to menopause specialists through virtual-care platforms such as \uc2 Maven Clinic\uc2 , \uc2 Progyny\uc2  and Carrot that focus on women's health.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , for example, began offering menopause benefits through \uc2 Maven\uc2  in 2023, including access to specialists.\par \par \uc2 In a \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  post, Maureen Sanders, a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  executive and 25-year veteran of the company, shared how she came close to quitting because specialists including a neurologist couldn't identify the source of her severe brain fog. It was only after a presentation at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  about menopause benefits offered that she connected with a specialist and received successful hormone treatment. (She declined to comment for this article.)\par \par \uc2 Some women, meanwhile, are turning to their peers for comfort and advice -- often with the help of their employers. A nurse at the Mayo Clinic's Center for Women's Health recently started an internal chat group for employees to discuss ways to manage menopause-related symptoms and within the first week had 300 members. \uc2 HSBC Holdings\uc2 ' HSBC U.K. has on-site and virtual "menopause cafes" for peer support. And employees at \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  have access to virtual peer-support groups led by menopause-trained facilitators.\par \par \uc2 For other women, it's something as simple as dedicated quiet rooms with air vents and fans that can make all of the difference.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Cess, an editorial director at the vitamin-and-supplement company Olly, reserves a quiet room each Tuesday, her in-office day, after commuting on San Francisco's packed public transit. She says she "makes a beeline" to the quiet room where she cools down and refocuses. It takes her 20 to 30 minutes to calm her body so she can "be present and ready to collaborate."\par \par \uc2 "It's become a mandatory part of my routine," said Cess, age 41, whose perimenopause symptoms include brain fog, vertigo, anxiety, fatigue, insomnia and acute difficulty regulating her temperature. "I need that time to gather myself."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Tara Weiss is a writer in New York. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251013elad00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
