\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Unofficial Figures Show the Job Market Losing Steam\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier \par \uc2 500 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A host of alternative jobs data from Wall Street are pointing in the same direction: The U.S. labor market is losing steam.\par \par \uc2 These numbers are getting more attention while the federal government shutdown keeps the lights off at the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , delaying new data in an unsettled time for the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 In the federal data void, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  this week said it is seeing signs of rising unemployment and slowing job growth in customers' data. Private-equity company \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 , extrapolating from companies in which it owns stakes, said Tuesday it thinks overall U.S. jobs growth slid in September from an already weak official reading in August. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said its measure of labor-market tightness fell last month back to levels seen in 2015, indicating a tough landscape for job seekers.\par \par \uc2 A report from payroll processor ADP last week said U.S. employers shed private-sector jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 These and other alternative data sources have their drawbacks -- they often only cover a small share of the labor force, and using them to estimate the state of the broader job market can be tricky. These numbers don't always match with government data, though the government numbers themselves have seen some steep revisions lately as the \uc2 BLS\uc2  struggles with falling response rates to its surveys.\par \par \uc2 Still, the nongovernment numbers are telling the same basic story about a job market that has cooled since the spring: Few companies are hiring.\par \par \uc2 "The employment numbers have clearly deteriorated," said Jake Oubina, senior economist at \uc2 Piper Sandler\uc2 . Profit margins are down because of tariffs, and that makes companies less likely to hire, he said.\par \par \uc2 Economists and investors are closely watching nongovernment data while the federal shutdown delays important federal data releases, including a September jobs report that was supposed to come out last Friday. That report, one of the key U.S. economic indicators, helps set the course for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's interest- rate policy. The Fed last month lowered interest rates by a quarter percentage point and indicated more cuts are coming while citing weak hiring.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that few people are getting laid off while the unemployment rate, last released for August, remained low. \uc2 Piper Sandler\uc2  tallied state-level unemployment claims and found they remained low last week. Outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  said job-cut announcements fell in September.\par \par \uc2 Lower immigration means fewer jobs need to be added to keep unemployment steady, said \uc2 Jason Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 's head of global research and investment strategy. Considering this, the labor market is "probably pretty healthy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Carlyle\uc2 's own data suggest hiring has slowed significantly. The firm estimated that U.S. employers added 17,000 jobs in September, down from an already weak 22,000 in August. \uc2 Carlyle\uc2  draws its estimates from head-count and business volumes data among the firms where it has some ownership.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251010elaa0001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
