\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Outlook For Cuts Split Fed Last Month\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials were divided over how much farther they should lower interest rates when they approved their first reduction of the year last month, according to a written record of their meeting released Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Officials largely agreed a recent slowdown in job growth outweighed lingering concerns over sticky inflation when they cut their benchmark rate by a quarter-point last month, to a range between 4% and 4.25%.\par \par \uc2 Most thought "it would likely be appropriate to ease policy further over the remainder of the year," according to minutes of the Sept. 16-17 meeting. But the written account said a few thought a rate cut wasn't necessary last month or could have also supported holding rates steady.\par \par \uc2 The minutes indicated support for a larger half-point cut was limited to one official, Fed governor Stephen Miran, who dissented from the decision. Miran was appointed by President Trump and sworn in on the first day of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 A narrow majority of 19 officials penciled in at least two additional cuts this year, implying a tentative base case for consecutive reductions at the Fed's meetings this month and in December. But a substantial minority of seven penciled in no further reductions this year, highlighting a thorny consensus-building exercise facing Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The recent government shutdown has for now robbed officials of the economic data releases that could reconcile any disagreements. Often, the Fed minutes -- released with a three-week lag -- can be stale because intervening economic data releases have shed new light on the outlook in ways that resolve divisions.\par \par \uc2 Investors widely expect the Fed to cut interest rates by another quarter point at the next meeting, Oct. 28-29.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251009ela90001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
