\par\par \b \uc2 Shutdown Starting To Exact Its Toll on Business\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Simon, Theo Francis and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Businesses nationwide are starting to feel the pain, one week into the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown.\par \par \uc2 An Alameda, Calif., medical-device company postponed a planned spinoff, unable to pursue regulatory approvals. A Florida marketing company laid off five employees after receiving a stop-work order on a federal contract. A Minnesota subcontractor is trying to keep a half-dozen electricians working while it waits for a stalled government contract.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown is disrupting business as usual in many ways -- and reinforcing just how many private-sector operations depend on a federal bureaucracy humming on all cylinders. Government-backed small-business lending has ground to a halt. Agencies can't award or make payments on many new contracts. Scheduled workplace-safety inspections are on hold and so are many regulatory reviews.\par \par \uc2 Small government subcontractors such as Brian Butler's marketing-and-communications firm said they are already being hit and don't know when they will be paid for some work done or how long the shutdown will last.\par \par \uc2 "That's a very scary place to be in as an entrepreneur," said Butler, whose Lutz, Fla., firm, Vistra Communications, employs about 80 people and gets about three-quarters of its revenue from the federal government. Butler laid off five of his 80 employees last week after the shutdown triggered a stop-work order on a project. Butler paid the workers a week of salary and is covering the employees' share of health-insurance costs for the month.\par \par \uc2 "If I receive one, two, three more of these, I'm not sure I can do that for that many people," said Butler of the stop-work orders.\par \par \uc2 Curtailed operations at the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  delayed some business plans. \uc2 Vivani Medical\uc2 , a company developing drug implants, said it had to pause a planned spinoff of its brain-implant subsidiary since it couldn't continue with registering the spinoff's shares with the SEC. It had recently set Wednesday as the deal's record date -- when it would determine which existing shareholders would receive the right to shares in the new company, \uc2 Cortigent\uc2 . Vivani, which has a market capitalization of about $80 million, said it would set a new date once the SEC reopened.\par \par \uc2 Some economists worry the pain could spread to the economy if the stalemate stretches into weeks. The shutdown could cut annual economic growth by as much as 0.2 percentage point for every week it lasts, the consulting firm Oxford Economics estimates.\par \par \uc2 Much of that should be recovered when the shutdown ends, assuming the government makes good on delayed invoices and back pay for furloughed workers. Still, "the longer it goes on, the more of that hit to growth won't be recovered," said \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , lead U.S. economist at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 Vinco, a telecommunications and electrical subcontractor, learned just over a month ago that it was part of the winning bid for a Minneapolis-St. Paul Air Force Reserve building upgrade. But the shutdown has held up the paperwork needed to process the contract, which would employ six to eight skilled electricians at Vinco for about nine months.\par \par \uc2 Steve Anderson, president of the Forest Lake, Minn., company, said he will put the electricians on other jobs, even if doing so means overstaffing some projects. That will hurt profit margins at the 300-person company, which receives about 5% of its sales from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 If the shutdown stretches on longer, he said, Vinco might have to lay off workers -- or take less-profitable jobs to keep them. "There's such a demand for the specialty electric trades that, if you lay them off or send them home, you will lose them," Anderson said.\par \par \uc2 Electricians, plumbers and other field-service professionals frequently count on stable government work to counterbalance less-predictable residential business, said Didi Azaria, chief executive of \uc2 Workiz\uc2 , an online platform providing scheduling, invoicing and other services to such disciplines. About 40% of sales for its 127,000 customers are from commercial or government accounts, and shutdown-related delays are mounting.\par \par \uc2 "Every job is getting delayed, or they're not getting paid on time," Azaria said. "It creates a cash problem, meaning you cannot buy equipment, you cannot pay your employees."\par \par \uc2 Fire Starter Studios, a Santa Clarita, Calif., video-production company, typically gets about 50% of its revenue from federal contracts. Days before the budget impasse, Fire Starter completed the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  process that allows it to contract with government agencies at preset rates, an effort that took two years and cost about $10,000.\par \par \uc2 "Now we have nothing to show for it," said owner Rachel Klein, who employs anywhere from a dozen to 200 people at a time. "No new business now means no money in two, three or four months."\par \par \uc2 Some effects from the shutdown might take longer to surface. At the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 , which investigates workplace injuries and deaths, about 460 people are still working, without pay, out of a staff of 1,664, the Labor Department said. Contingency plans for shutdowns in the previous administration called for all of \uc2 OSHA\uc2 's inspectors to keep working, said Doug Parker, the \uc2 OSHA\uc2  head during the Biden administration. The current staffing level isn't "enough to respond to all of the complaints," he said. "It's going to prevent \uc2 OSHA\uc2  from intervening to protect workers."\par \par \uc2 According to the Labor Department's shutdown plan, the department will continue to monitor and respond to imminent threats to human life and continue inspections in high-hazard industries.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251009ela90001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
