\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Republicans Caution White House Against Shutdown Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Andrews, Meridith McGraw and Lindsay Wise \par \uc2 519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  and other senior GOP lawmakers have quietly advised the White House not to move forward with mass layoffs and sharp cuts to government assistance programs as the shutdown enters its second week, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 In recent conversations, Thune (R., S.D.) has counseled the president to attempt to limit the fallout from the shutdown for as long as possible, according to one of the people. Far-reaching government cuts and firings could backfire with the public, lawmakers have told the president's aides, warning that such moves could cause voters to blame Republicans for the shutdown, the people said.\par \par \uc2 "The only reason we are forced to have these conversations is because the Democrats shut down the government," White House spokeswoman \uc2 Abigail Jackson\uc2  said, adding that the best way to avoid consequences from the shutdown is to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 The Republican hand-wringing reflects discomfort among some in the party over the president's shutdown strategy. Soon after government funding lapsed last week, Trump said the shutdown gave him an "unprecedented opportunity" to make cuts at agencies. White House officials have said they are considering firing thousands of federal workers and have raised the possibility that some workers won't get back pay.\par \par \uc2 The White House strategy is aimed at putting pressure on Democrats to reach a deal to reopen the government. But Democrats have refused to budge, arguing that Republicans first need to agree to extend expiring healthcare subsidies upon which millions of Americans rely.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that the White House is listening to Republicans' concerns. The administration hasn't moved forward with the layoffs that the president has been promising, though Trump said this week he might do so if the shutdown doesn't end soon. The president's advisers have taken steps to lessen the impact of the shutdown, including pledging to use tariff revenue to keep money flowing to a food program for women, infants and children. And Trump told reporters on Wednesday that most federal workers would get back pay.\par \par \uc2 Trump rattled some GOP lawmakers when he said earlier this week that he was talking to Democrats about a deal to extend enhanced Affordable Care Act subsidies. Democratic lawmakers quickly said no such talks were under way and Trump later clarified on social media that he was "happy to work with" the Democrats, but only after the government reopens.\par \par \uc2 The mixed messaging from the president has frustrated some Republicans, who have said they are eager for Trump to chart a clear path forward for dealing with the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in Congress said the party should unite around a clear message: that Democrats are holding the government hostage over policy demands, and that any negotiations should happen after they vote to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 The simmering tension among Republicans comes as Democrats, who for months have struggled to recover from their losses in the recent election, have united around a shutdown message focused on healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251009ela90001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
