\par\par \b \uc2 At \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 , the Staff Acts Like Gig Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Bousquette \par \uc2 677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 , the notion of a traditional full-time job is becoming more irrelevant, as the bank looks to remake its workforce for the artificial-intelligence age.\par \par \uc2 Its employees still have job titles and descriptions, but within the firm they are acting more like gig workers, taking on ad-hoc projects that may have little or nothing to do with the job for which they were hired.\par \par \uc2 The more-flexible approach -- mediated by an in-house "talent marketplace" -- is critical to redeploying human capital that can be far more productive with AI tools, as well as speeding the pace of new AI deployments, said \uc2 Tanuj Kapilashrami\uc2 , chief strategy and talent officer.\par \par \uc2 "The idea of a traditional job being a currency of work is going to become less and less relevant," she said. "You don't have to think of an individual by the job title or the job description, but you think of an individual as a collection of skills."\par \par \uc2 Companies across the board are facing a misalignment between the way traditional corporate-organization charts stratify skills and getting the most out of AI. They are aware AI will change jobs -- eradicating many while creating new ones -- but they struggle to figure out how best to use the employees they have and reskill them for new roles. Companies like \uc2 Moderna\uc2  say they have been reworking teams not just around the capabilities of the people on staff, but also around what can be completed by AI tools.\par \par \uc2 The idea of internal skills marketplaces is gaining traction, according to Hatim Rahman, associate professor of management and organizations at the Kellogg School of Management at \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 . Typically, the main benefit is employee retention: Workers who can flex and learn new skills across an organization are less likely to leave for those opportunities, he said. The marketplaces also have been "incidentally helpful" in reassigning work as AI rushes in, he added.\par \par \uc2 The gig work on \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 's marketplace, which was set up in 2020, helped create more than $8.5 million in value by enabling projects that might have been sidelined in the past due to understaffing or a lengthy hiring process, said Kapilashrami.\par \par \uc2 On the talent marketplace, workers can post and apply for gigs in things like basic programming or communications. Currently 60% of the bank's employees globally are active on the platform, nabbing gigs they can spend up to eight hours on within their regular workweek. While they don't make extra money, staff see it as an opportunity to network and build new skills, especially important at a time the labor market is shifting, Kapilashrami said.\par \par \uc2 For the company, the marketplace has been a way to best use the skills it has instead of overhiring.\par \par \uc2 "As new pieces of work are coming up, as opposed to very quickly going, 'How many new [full-time employees] do we need?' We are having a very clear conversation on: Is it about building skills? Is it about buying skills? Is it about borrowing skills?" she said.\par \par \uc2 The talent marketplace also is allowing the company to move quickly on building and rolling out new uses for AI in areas like wealth management, advising clients and marketing.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, core AI engineering isn't a skill most white-collar employees have. But Kapilashrami pointed out there are other skills they do have that can improve and speed the adoption of AI, among them expertise on regulation, compliance, ethics or particular industries.\par \par \uc2 Companies that want to move quickly on AI have to think holistically about the skills of the workforce they have before knowing where they need to hire -- especially since landing top tech talent these days is no easy feat, she said.\par \par \uc2 "There is a massive skill shortage," she said. "And if businesses feel that they are just going to be able to buy their way into how business models are changing, it's just not going to happen."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Isabelle Bousquette writes for WSJ Pro's CIO Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251009ela90000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
