\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Warehouse Vacancies in the U.S. Steady\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark R. Long \par \uc2 557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The amount of vacant warehouse space in the U.S. held steady around an 11-year high in the third quarter, but it didn't expand for the first time in three years as demand rose and the amount of newly built space continued to fall.\par \par \uc2 The average warehouse vacancy rate across the U.S. was 7.1% in the three months ended Sept. 30, unchanged from the second quarter, according to a report from \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 . The net increase in occupied space from the second quarter was 45.1 million square feet, up 30.4% from the so-called absorption figure seen in the April-to-June period, the commercial real-estate services firm said.\par \par \uc2 Demand for warehouse space surged earlier this year as retailers, manufacturers and wholesalers built up stockpiles of inventory ahead of President Trump's anticipated tariffs, then pared back overseas orders and paused leasing decisions as the duties were announced.\par \par \uc2 The market stabilized somewhat in the third quarter, with demand from more traditional drivers such as the manufacturing and auto sectors, and a still soft but improving housing market, said Jason Tolliver, Cushman's president of logistics and industrial commercial real-estate services. Atlanta, central New Jersey and San Diego were among the markets where occupancy rose, reversing previous quarters' losses.\par \par \uc2 The industrial real-estate sector has struggled for years with rising availability due to weak demand and an influx of newly built space, following a period of frenzied expansion in warehouse construction and leasing during the pandemic that drove vacancy down below 3%.\par \par \uc2 In the third quarter, the amount of warehouse space completed was down 14.6% from the previous three months at 63.6 million square feet, which is nearly 33% lower than a year earlier. The amount of space under construction nudged 1% higher from the second quarter, but was down more than 13% from the third quarter of 2024.\par \par \uc2 "On the supply side, we continue to see very conservative development," Tolliver said.\par \par \uc2 The South accounted for 44% of new space delivered, and bespoke construction continued to rise, with build-to-suit projects making up 31% of completions to date this year, up 22% from the same period last year. Of the new buildings under construction, 39% are build-to-suit, the report says.\par \par \uc2 The national average asking rent was $10.10 a square foot in the third quarter, flat from the preceding three months and up 1.7% from the same period a year earlier, according to Cushman. Rents fell in the West and Northeast and grew modestly in the South and Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Tolliver said he was seeing a broader range of companies leasing and touring space, from branded businesses to third-party logistics firms hired by retailers and others to help them navigate increasingly complex supply chains and trade policies.\par \par \uc2 Industrial projects spurred by 2022's Inflation Reduction Act have been coming online, buoying demand, with fresh needs for space coming from reshoring and nearshoring. More businesses from Europe and Asia are seeking to get closer to their end consumers in the U.S., he added.\par \par \uc2 "I have a high degree of confidence that the fourth quarter will be better than the third," Tolliver said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mark R. Long writes for WSJ Pro's Logistics Report.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6645
