\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Kept Cuts Under Wraps --- Chain's 'Project Bloom' was months in the making and days in execution\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's Seattle headquarters, it was known as "Project Bloom."\par \par \uc2 Launched earlier this year, the hush-hush undertaking evaluated thousands of the company's coffee shops across North America on profitability, and the experience of customers and baristas.\par \par \uc2 It led to a roller coaster of a week in late September, in which the company abruptly closed hundreds of stores and laid off thousands of employees. It was \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's second round of corporate layoffs in less than a year under Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  and among the chain's biggest closing of cafes since 2008.\par \par \uc2 "The reality is the business has not performed and we needed to recognize that aspect," Niccol said in an internal company forrum, a recording of which was reviewed by The Wall Street Journal. "We needed to make some changes."\par \par \uc2 In a year at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's helm, Niccol has overhauled its workforce and operations. He has remade the C-suite, hiring new executives to head operations, finances and development, while technology and coffee-sourcing leaders have retired in recent weeks. Menus and advertising are also evolving.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the cafe closures and staff cuts, a company long known for its sprawling growth has abruptly shrunk.\par \par \uc2 Niccol and his team have said major changes were needed to rebound from six consecutive quarters of declining same-store sales, and to build a more sustainable company that can pave the way to growth. Americans are recoiling at rising restaurant and coffee prices and eating out less, making \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's turnaround more challenging.\par \par \uc2 In April, Niccol said \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  would review its global store portfolio and pipeline of new locations as construction and renovation costs climbed. He said in July that the review of North American stores would wrap up in two months.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  regularly reviews its store portfolio, but this assessment was more ambitious. The company owned 11,450 stores in the U.S. and Canada as of June 29, and licensees operated 7,300 others at locations like airports, hotels and malls.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  divisions were involved in assessing stores. Executives wanted the review on underperforming locations done by the end of the company's fiscal year on Sept. 29.\par \par \uc2 The company's board signed off on the store-closure and corporate-layoff plans last week.\par \par \uc2 Early on Thursday, Sept. 25, baristas and corporate employees received a companywide message from Niccol announcing the layoffs, store closures and reinvestment priorities.\par \par \uc2 "These steps are to reinforce what we see is working and prioritize our resources against them," Niccol said in the message.\par \par \uc2 Later that morning, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  notified managers in cafes marked for closure. Regional corporate leaders convened calls with managers at stores remaining open to walk them through the plans.\par \par \uc2 The company advised store managers that they would need to help dispose of suddenly unneeded inventory, like cups, oat milk and lemonade. They also might hear from baristas looking for jobs.\par \par \uc2 "Transparently, this is a really quick turnaround," said one regional \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  leader on a call, a recording of which was reviewed by the Journal.\par \par \uc2 In closing locations, store signs went up to notify customers. The company provided a QR code to help them find a new one.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, baristas at closing stores received emails about their jobs.\par \par \uc2 "We regret to inform you that we no longer have a position available for you at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ," read one of the emails. "We wish you all the best in your future endeavors."\par \par \uc2 Some customers vented online about the closures, and questioned their \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  loyalty. Baristas posted tearful TikTok videos after receiving layoff notices. Some of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's tightknit alumni offered assistance for those laid off, and others criticized the cuts and closures.\par \par \uc2 "A huge impact to the soul of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ," Christine McHugh, a former vice president who spent 27 years at the company before leaving in 2017, said in a \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  post last week.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it hopes to hire back baristas as it resumes opening new stores this fiscal year, and said it is giving laid-off baristas an industry-leading severance package.\par \par \uc2 Some investors were pleased to see Niccol take action on \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's expenses, and that store closures were occurring quickly. Since \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  announced the layoffs and store closures in late September, its stock has gained about 2.6%.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Niccol said in a video message to employees that he hoped the broad staffing cuts and store closures were over. Shutting down stores like its flagship Seattle Reserve Roastery location was painful, but they were losing money, he said, and closing underperforming locations helps allocate resources to other cafes.\par \par \uc2 The following day Niccol held a forum at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's headquarters with employees and operations chief \uc2 Mike Grams\uc2 , to further explain his decisions and map out the road ahead. The last days were tough, Niccol said, but so were the months leading up to the closures.\par \par \uc2 Efforts over the past year to reinvest in cafe service and atmosphere have been boosting business, executives said, with the chain directing hundreds of millions of dollars toward cafe staffing and improvements.\par \par \uc2 Niccol said sales this week were strong, and the company was set to introduce new food and beverages, and a better app.\par \par \uc2 One morning early last week, Niccol said, he visited a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  location to check in on a line of protein-infused beverages making their debut that day. He ordered around eight of them, and liked what he saw and tasted.\par \par \uc2 "One day isn't a trend, but one day is how you start a trend," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251006ela60001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
