\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Healthcare, Education Groups Sue Over Visa Fee\b0\par\par\uc2 By Louise Radnofsky and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 235 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A healthcare-staffing business and labor unions are suing the Trump administration over new fees for H-1B visas, saying the president doesn't have the authority to demand $100,000 for every new application in the popular program for foreign professionals.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, filed in federal court in the Northern District of California on Friday, is the first major legal challenge to the fees and asks the court to block the government from proceeding with them.\par \par \uc2 The coalition, led by recruitment firm Global Nurse Force, argues that the change needs the approval of Congress. It also says that the Trump administration violated the Administrative Procedure Act by acting unreasonably and skipping the normal process.\par \par \uc2 The skilled-worker visa program has long been an immigration flashpoint among Trump supporters. Tech-industry leaders argue that H-1Bs are critical to the U.S. being an innovation leader, while other MAGA voices have said the program gives companies an excuse to hire foreign workers over Americans.\par \par \uc2 The H-1B program is a main pathway for foreign professionals to enter the U.S., and it is also used by students hoping to remain in the country. It costs $215 to enter an applicant into the lottery for an H-1B visa and more than $5,000 in fees to file an application.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251004ela40001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
