\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  Gets What It Paid For\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As if tariffs and inflation aren't enough, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  may face a strike at its largest U.S. plant. A work stoppage can't be what the company envisioned last year when it welcomed a campaign to unionize the plant, and the standoff is a lesson for other car makers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW)\uc2  announced last weekend that support for a strike is "overwhelming" among its members at VW's plant in Chattanooga, Tenn., based on pledge cards workers filled out before a formal vote can be called. The strike threat emerged after a year of contract talks without an agreement, though VW says it has moved a long way toward a deal.\par \par \uc2 The company's "best and final" offer would raise wages 20% over four years, along with one-time bonuses of up to $5,500 and another 8% annual bonus for good attendance. It also includes an annual cost-of-living adjustment of up to 45 cents an hour. The union wants a 24% raise to bring wages in line with those at the Big Three auto makers, as well as bigger corporate contributions to health and retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 The strike threat means VW is likely to pay either in lost output or a richer-than-expected contract. A one-week stoppage could cost millions of dollars in unshipped units, and the costs compound the longer it lasts. But accepting the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's terms would boost permanent costs and erase much of the margin that makes the plant competitive. That could eliminate jobs in the long run.\par \par \uc2 VW's dilemma is the result of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's successful campaign to organize the plant in April 2024, after workers had rejected the union in 2014 and 2019. The biggest difference last year was the lack of company opposition. VW declined to advise employees of the union's long-term costs, and 73% of workers welcomed the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Company culture is one part of the story. The German auto maker is used to working with unions back home, which take part in its governance and are usually less combative than their American peers.\par \par \uc2 But politics may also have pushed VW to roll over. Thirty-three Senate Democrats wrote a letter in January 2024 to every non-union auto maker in the U.S., suggesting the companies would lose electric-vehicle subsidies if they opposed union campaigns. VW, which builds an electric SUV in Chattanooga, may have decided that fighting the union would be the costlier move. Now the EV subsidies are going away in any case thanks to the GOP budget bill and Trump Administration orders.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hoped their threat would help the \uc2 UAW\uc2  win more plants in the South, where many foreign auto makers and startups have set up to take advantage of right-to-work laws. Yet Mercedes workers at a plant near Tuscaloosa, Ala., snubbed the union last year, as did Hyundai workers in Montgomery. Both companies campaigned against the union, and employees sided with the companies.\par \par \uc2 Democrats lost the power to steer companies toward unions when Republicans claimed the White House and Senate, which may simplify the question for non-union auto makers. The decision is in workers' hands, but company engagement makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Time to Protect Workers' Pay" -- WSJ Oct. 10, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251003ela300019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
