\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Walmart\uc2  Wants More Developers, AI Agents to Automate Their Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Belle Lin \par \uc2 716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's global tech chief said the retail giant will continue to hire top engineering talent, even as it builds more AI agents to automate their work.\par \par \uc2 The company's focus on engineering and AI agents comes as Chief Executive \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2  said last week that "AI is going to change literally every job." While some jobs and tasks at \uc2 Walmart\uc2  will be eliminated, McMillon said, others will be created.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  is still bringing on new developers. For instance, the retailer last month created an "agent developer" position, which calls for the employee to "deploy agents that automate complex workflows."\par \par \uc2 "We continue to adjust our workforce given the new realities, but we're still working very hard to find the right talent in the market," said Sravana Karnati, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's executive vice president of global technology platforms. "Good engineers are always needed."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Walmart\uc2  representative added that the company will "continue to invest in upskilling, recruiting top-tier engineers with deep expertise, and redeploying talent to areas that demand uniquely human strengths."\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's focus on hiring top engineers comes amid a contraction within the wider IT jobs market. Jobs in tech have been harder to land with ongoing economic uncertainty -- causing employers to think twice about all but the most sterling of candidates, or those who have familiarity with AI. Many of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's open engineering positions call for candidates with AI-related skills.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives also said last week that the company's global workforce will stay roughly flat over the next three years, even as its mix of jobs will change significantly.\par \par \uc2 That could mean more of a focus on AI agents as \uc2 Walmart\uc2  sorts through how its roughly 2.1 million employees will be affected. Those agents, which are AI bots that can autonomously perform tasks on behalf of humans, have already helped boost productivity for \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's software engineers, Karnati said.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  declined to say how much it is spending on its technology investments, and what kind of financial return it is seeing from them.\par \par \uc2 In August, the company unveiled a "super agent" for engineers called Wibey, which Karnati said helps unify the more than 200 AI agents \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's tens of thousands developers have built so far.\par \par \uc2 Now, using the Wibey super agent, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's engineers can register their newly created agents with the system and advertise their agent's capabilities for other employees to use, Karnati said.\par \par \uc2 One set of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's AI agents is automating the process of complying with accessibility requirements, or ensuring that \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's apps and website are accessible for people with disabilities. That has helped \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's engineers automatically identify 60% of software bugs in accessibility compliance and automatically fix 95% of them -- leading to an improvement of eight times in productivity in that area, according to Karnati.\par \par \uc2 AI also is helping \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's engineers get up to speed and modernize its legacy software code -- some of it written decades ago, and some of it written in computer mainframe language. Before using AI tools, Karnati said developers needed to seek out senior engineers for their expertise in its code base, resulting in a lot of time spent chasing down answers.\par \par \uc2 In addition to using AI agents, more than 95% of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's engineers are using AI coding assistant tools like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's \uc2 GitHub\uc2  Copilot and JetBrains, according to Karnati.\par \par \uc2 In the future, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's AI agents will become sophisticated enough to not just interact with humans, but also interact and collaborate with each other, Karnati said.\par \par \uc2 That will create new challenges for the company, including how to manage humans alongside their digital counterparts. One scenario Karnati imagined is that a human engineering manager lets AI agents work overnight to solve a problem, and returns in the morning to inspect the work of the bots.\par \par \uc2 So while \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's engineers are becoming more productive with AI, Karnati said that also means they are discovering more problems for more humans to solve.\par \par \uc2 "We're not at a point where we can let an agent run loose and let it solve all kinds of problems," he said. "We will still keep humans in the loop."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Belle Lin writes for WSJ's Pro CIO Vertical.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251003ela30001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
