\par\par \b \uc2 Parties Fight to Cast Shutdown Blame --- Some in Congress seek path to accord as White House eyes layoffs this week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Wise, Olivia Beavers and Alex Leary \par \uc2 1,009 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Hours into the government shutdown, the White House issued new threats, congressional leaders were digging in, and some rank-and-file Democrats and Republicans were searching for a way out.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  (R., S.D.) and Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  said they are open to talking to Democrats about extending expiring healthcare subsidies but won't negotiate as long as the government is closed. Democratic leaders said they won't agree to reopen the government until their healthcare demands -- at least some of them -- are met.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown that started at 12:01 a.m. Wednesday ratchets up the pressure on both sides. Hundreds of thousands of federal workers are being put on furlough, and the White House said layoff announcements would be made soon. Top Republicans and Democrats took to cable news and news conferences Wednesday on Capitol Hill to point fingers at the other side.\par \par \uc2 Both parties see a potential path to ending the shutdown, centered on extending enhanced Affordable Care Act subsidies, whose expiration at the end of this year would lead to higher healthcare costs for more than 20 million people. But any such deal could raise objections from hard-line Republicans or the left wing of the Democratic caucus, if it was seen as giving in too easily, and President Trump's support would be critical. Both parties said the other side needs to blink first.\par \par \uc2 "I am prepared to go to the Capitol right now to talk to Democrats" about ACA premiums, Vance said on Fox News. But only if the government is open, he said. "You can't reward this exercise in hostage-taking," he said.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs across federal agencies will begin later this week, White House budget director \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2  told House Republicans on a call Wednesday, according to participants. He didn't give an indication of how many cuts there will be, but one House Republican said he indicated they would be "consequential." Democrats and unions have challenged the legality of such cuts.\par \par \uc2 Vought also indicated that the government's food-assistance program, known as WIC, is expected to run out of funds in the next week or two, depending on the state. On X, Vought said he was freezing billions of dollars in funding for New York infrastructure projects and energy projects in over a dozen Democratic-leaning states, moves Democrats decried as politically driven.\par \par \uc2 Government websites posted partisan messages saying the shutdown was Democrats' fault.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York, the chamber's Democratic leader, blamed Trump and fellow Republicans for the shutdown because he said they had refused to protect Americans' healthcare. Democrats, while focused on protecting ACA subsidies, have also demanded that Congress roll back cuts to Medicaid, putting the overall price tag at more than $1 trillion over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 "Republicans tried to bully us and it's clear they can't. They don't have the votes," Schumer said on the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 Among Senate Republicans, there is growing support for a fix. Sen. \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  (R., S.D.) outlined a possible deal involving a yearlong extension with a phaseout over the next few years that could include income caps to limit eligibility and other changes to address fraud and waste. He said it would take time to reach any consensus and draft legislation, and urged Democrats to approve the GOP's seven-week stopgap bill in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 "Give us the 45 days, nothing gets hurt by giving the 45 days, and let's get this deal put together," he said.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (R., Mo.) said Republicans can't just do nothing and let the subsidies expire. "Like, in Missouri, the premiums almost double. Oh, it's bad," he said.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Jeanne Shaheen (D., N.H.) said there were bipartisan talks between rank-and-file senators and that she was "encouraged that there are so many people on both sides that think we can work this out." She said she would accept as part of a deal assurances from GOP leadership about negotiating an extension of the expiring healthcare subsidies in the future.\par \par \uc2 But for other Democrats, that isn't enough.\par \par \uc2 "There has to be something enforceable," said Sen. Peter Welch (D., Vt.) emphasizing that Republican colleagues would have to uphold their end.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers said any deal would require Trump's endorsement, and it isn't clear where he stands. Schumer has said the president seemed to be surprised when Democrats presented him with their concerns about the expiration of ACA tax credits at their White House meeting this week.\par \par \uc2 Some moderate House Republicans have proposed a yearlong extension of the subsidies, until after the midterms. Hard-liners in the House Freedom Caucus have made it clear they would oppose any deal that increased spending. Freedom Caucus Chair Andy Harris (R., Md.) has warned that an ACA deal could hurt House Speaker Mike Johnson (R., La.) politically.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democratic leaders aren't confident that Johnson will put any deal on subsidies up for a vote, according to a person familiar with their thinking. They see the speaker -- due to his job insecurity -- as the primary obstacle to a deal. But they also believe Trump could decide at any moment to back a deal, and the standoff would be over.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats have largely remained united. But the White House and congressional Republicans are counting on centrists to crack in the coming days and deliver the votes the GOP needs to pass their stopgap bill funding the government through Nov. 21.\par \par \uc2 Three centrist members of the Democratic caucus voted with Republicans on Tuesday night to try to avert a shutdown, but Republicans would need at least five more Democratic defectors to reach the 60 votes needed under the Senate's filibuster rule. Thune plans to hold repeated votes on the GOP's bill into the weekend to squeeze the centrists.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251002ela20001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
