\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: What to Expect As Funding Lapses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anvee Bhutani \par \uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Funding for federal agencies lapsed beginning at 12:01 a.m. on Wednesday, after Congress was unable to pass a stopgap bill to prevent a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 In a shutdown, government offices continue essential work, but tasks deemed nonessential come to a halt, paychecks stop and many workers are furloughed until Congress passes new funding.\par \par \uc2 Will Social Security payments continue?\par \par \uc2 Social Security, Medicare and Medicaid will continue sending out payments, though benefit verification and card issuance could pause.\par \par \uc2 I'm a federal worker. Will I still get paid?\par \par \uc2 Yes, but not right away. While most federal employees, regardless of their essential status, won't be paid during a shutdown, a 2019 law guarantees automatic back pay once the shutdown concludes. Private contractors who work with federal agencies and are furloughed during a shutdown aren't guaranteed back pay. The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimated that about 750,000 federal employees could be furloughed in a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Are some federal workers being fired?\par \par \uc2 White House budget chief \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  told House GOP lawmakers on Wednesday that layoffs will begin later this week. Vought previously asked agencies to draw up plans for layoffs during the shutdown, and President Trump voiced support for further staffing cuts and spending reductions. Permanent job cuts increase the chance of an economic impact as affected people cut back on spending.\par \par \uc2 How could it hit travel?\par \par \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  employees and air-traffic controllers would work without pay, but travelers could see longer security lines or delays, especially if employees skip unpaid work. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  has said its trains will continue running, but larger infrastructure projects might be delayed.\par \par \uc2 Are passport and visa services stopped?\par \par \uc2 Passport services should continue but could have delays. People are able to opt in to use expedited services if necessary. H-1B and other visa-processing services might also slow down or grind to a halt.\par \par \uc2 Will national parks close?\par \par \uc2 As of late Tuesday, the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  aimed to keep roads, trails and memorials generally open. Parks that collect visitor fees at entrance gates will use those balances to help provide visitor services, like collecting trash and maintaining restrooms. In Washington, D.C., the Smithsonian museums and the National Zoo will use prior-year funds to remain open at least through Oct. 6, according to a website notice.\par \par \uc2 Will SNAP and other programs continue?\par \par \uc2 Yes. The Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, also known as food stamps, has contingency plans to temporarily continue operations in case of a lapse in funding, though it is unclear how long this would be sustainable. WIC, the Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants and Children, meanwhile, could pause its programs if it doesn't receive funding soon. Americans who are interacting with federal agencies for support, such as getting food-stamp benefits, could experience delays.\par \par \uc2 What about ICE?\par \par \uc2 Most ICE employees are considered essential and will continue working, according to recently released contingency plans from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . This means immigration enforcement and deportation operations would continue, including countrywide arrests and raids.\par \par \uc2 How about military personnel and veterans?\par \par \uc2 Active-duty military personnel are considered essential employees and thus are required to work during a government shutdown. Veterans Health Administration and Veterans Benefits Administration services are unlikely to be seriously affected, though some workers will be furloughed.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 's core functions would continue, though grant approvals would be put on pause, including new mortgages that require flood insurance. The National Weather Service will continue to provide weather forecasts, but all tours and outreach activities will be paused.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251002ela200012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
