\par\par \b \uc2 World News: U.S. Pulls Support for Haiti Textiles --- Trade program's end imperils embattled clothing exporters employing thousands\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kejal Vyas \par \uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For nearly two decades, the U.S. provided a lifeline to Haiti: duty-free textile imports that drew American apparel manufacturers and created tens of thousands of jobs in the Western Hemisphere's poorest country. As of Wednesday, that lifeline is no more.\par \par \uc2 The expiration of the so-called HOPE/HELP trade program is expected to crush what is left of Haiti's biggest industry, which accounted for 90% of exports in a country mired in a gang war that has stoked hunger, generated a refugee crisis and left the government teetering on the brink of collapse.\par \par \uc2 U.S. lawmakers and Haitian businesses said the end of the program will deepen poverty and bolster gang recruitment while costing jobs in factories that produce clothing for well-known brands -- including Hanes, Calvin Klein, Gap and \uc2 Victoria's Secret\uc2  -- as the industry relocates to Asia. The development could force desperate Haitians to flee to the Dominican Republic or other nations.\par \par \uc2 "Without those jobs, we are going to see more people left on the streets, more people drawn to crime and gangs," said Fernando Capellan, president of the Codevi industrial park along Haiti's border with the Dominican Republic, where some 18,000 of Haiti's 26,000 textile jobs are concentrated.\par \par \uc2 Haitian business leaders have been lobbying the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  for months for an extension of the legislation, which had enjoyed bipartisan support. But policies that stimulate jobs outside the U.S. have become a harder sell in the midst of the Trump administration's protectionist agenda.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody says 'yes, yes, yes,' but then nothing happens," said Georges Sassine, a veteran Haitian textile executive.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick (D., Fla.) said ending the program in Haiti would "push the country deeper into crisis."\par \par \uc2 "The future of Haiti and the security of the United States depend on it," she added.\par \par \uc2 President Trump this year said he is seeking to return manufacturing to the U.S., but his priority is to attract makers of military equipment and technology, not the textile industry.\par \par \uc2 The State Department, the \uc2 Office of the U.S. Trade Representative\uc2  and the White House didn't respond to requests to comment.\par \par \uc2 The HOPE/HELP initiative began in 2006 and expanded after the devastating 2010 earthquake. It was designed to promote business in Haiti while offering incentives so American apparel makers could locate production close to the U.S. instead of operating in Asia, where much of the clothing industry had gone.\par \par \uc2 Support from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and multilateral financial institutions such as the \uc2 Inter-American Development Bank\uc2  helped the industry take root. One center, Caracol in northern Haiti, opened in 2012 with $300 million in funding from the U.S. and development banks. As recently as 2021, the apparel industry employed some 60,000 workers in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 While Caracol was designed to accommodate tens of thousands of employees, today it employs only about 2,000 because of Haiti's security problems and the uncertainty over the preferential trade program.\par \par \uc2 "This will turn into a total white elephant," said Capellan, president of the industrial park near the Dominican Republic.\par \par \uc2 The expiration of the preferential program means U.S. importers of Haitian-made clothing will be subject to import duties between 20% and 30%, in addition to the 10% "reciprocal tariff" leveled by the Trump administration against many trade partners.\par \par \uc2 The legislation had provided duty-free entry into the U.S. and generated economic benefits to both sides, business advocates said. Cotton and other fabrics from the U.S. were used in Haiti to make the finished products shipped to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Much of the revenue generated by Haitian labor was then spent buying goods from the U.S., including some $260 million in rice from Louisiana and nearly $500 million in fuel in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. ran a trade surplus of nearly $600 million with Haiti last year.\par \par \uc2 The looming economic blow comes as Haiti's beleaguered government struggles to contain gangs that have taken over nearly the entire capital, Port-au-Prince. In recent months, the government teamed up with \uc2 Erik Prince\uc2 , the founder and controversial former leader of Blackwater, to loosen the gangs' hold on roads, fuel depots and commercial ports.\par \par \uc2 Those efforts could be in vain if Haiti's local economy is pulverized, making recovery efforts more challenging. Uncertainty over the future of HOPE/HELP had already led many multinationals this year to transfer production to factories in China, Bangladesh, Indonesia and Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251002ela20000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
