\par\par \b \uc2 Federal Shutdown Begins As Talks Fall Apart --- Last-minute funding plans fail in Senate; Trump expected to seek big job cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Andrews and Lindsay Ellis \par \uc2 977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Government funding lapsed early Wednesday morning after the White House and lawmakers failed to reach a spending deal, triggering a shutdown that is expected to halt some federal services and put hundreds of thousands of federal workers on furlough.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown could be more consequential than those that have happened in the past. Trump administration officials have said they plan to use the shutdown to reduce the size of the government, moving to cut jobs across agencies. That effort is already being challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 The funding lapse will have far-reaching ramifications. For the duration of the shutdown, the Labor Department is expected to pause the release of economic data -- likely including a highly anticipated jobs report that was set to be made public on Friday. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  will stop analyzing surveillance data for reportable diseases. Many planned workplace safety inspections will be canceled. And workers won't trim grass and install headstones at national cemeteries.\par \par \uc2 Senate lawmakers failed on Tuesday evening to agree to a measure to fund the government amid deep divisions between the parties. Republicans can't pass a funding plan without some Democratic support.\par \par \uc2 Republicans put forward a seven-week stopgap bill to keep the government funded, arguing that it would buy lawmakers time to hash out their differences and craft full-year spending bills. Democrats opposed the measure, calling for restoring hundreds of billions of dollars in healthcare funding, including enhanced Affordable Care Act subsidies that expire this year.\par \par \uc2 Both parties moved quickly to blame their opponents for the shutdown, with federal agencies sending emails to workers criticizing congressional Democrats for opposing the stopgap funding measure backed by Republicans. A banner across the top of the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2 's website asserted that the "Radical Left" is responsible for the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 "The Republicans can end the shutdown by giving back healthcare to the American people," Sen. Mazie Hirono (D., Hawaii) said.\par \par \uc2 Voters have historically blamed the party in power for closing the government. But the political fallout will likely depend on the length and scale of the shutdown, which comes more than a year before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers aren't expected to return to Washington until next week and any compromise funding proposal would need their approval. Congress hasn't passed any appropriations bills, so unlike during the 34-day partial shutdown that ended in 2019, no government offices have been funded.\par \par \uc2 The Office of Management and Budget has asked agencies to consider reductions in force for employees whose work doesn't align with the president's priorities and isn't funded by other revenue streams. "We can cut large numbers of people," Trump said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to agencies, the OMB said employees are expected to report to work to carry out shutdown activities. "It is unclear how long Democrats will maintain their untenable posture, making the duration of a shutdown difficult to predict," the letter said.\par \par \uc2 Two unions representing government workers sued the Trump administration on Tuesday, arguing that plans for mass layoffs during a shutdown are unlawful.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown comes amid an already wobbly labor market, particularly in Washington, D.C., Virginia and Maryland. If the shutdown stretches for weeks or months, those areas, which are home to hundreds of thousands of federal employees, could see a lag in the local economy or more pressure on community services such as food banks.\par \par \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , Trump's budget chief, asked agencies last week to submit plans laying out how they would scale back services during a shutdown. An analysis of those plans by the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimated that 750,000 federal employees could be furloughed.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown will slow or halt government functions across agencies. The \uc2 Office for Civil Rights\uc2  at the Education Department won't investigate claims of discrimination in schools. The CDC won't offer guidance to states about opioid overdoses and diabetes or HIV prevention.\par \par \uc2 All but one staffer in the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which is tasked with collecting jobs data, will be furloughed. The \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  won't meet for peer-review research, nor will it admit new patients at its clinical center.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department will furlough tens of thousands of employees, according to its shutdown plan. Most services and activities of the \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  and the \uc2 International Trade Administration\uc2  will stop.\par \par \uc2 Many elective surgeries and other routine procedures at \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  medical and dental facilities will be paused. Veterans transitioning into civilian life also won't be able to access related health programs.\par \par \uc2 Certain government functions deemed critical, including medical care at Veterans Affairs hospitals, will continue. Though nearly 85% of the Office of Federal Student Aid will be furloughed, collections on student debt will proceed, according to agency plans. Staff will continue to release loans and Pell Grants to students. The \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2  will keep staff on hand to ensure benefits are paid, the agency said. Most of the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 's 25,000 employees will continue working.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  employees and air-traffic controllers will work without pay.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 's roads, trails and memorials will generally remain open, according to a person familiar with the shutdown plans.\par \par \uc2 Furloughed federal employees won't receive paychecks for the duration of the shutdown. In years past, they have received back pay.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2 , a nonprofit that advocates for federal workers, estimates that more than 200,000 employees have left the government this year. The Trump administration offered a buyout that pushed tens of thousands of employees off government payrolls this week. Agencies laid off many workers as well.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251001ela100025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
