\par\par \b \uc2 Oil Giants Set Path To Get Leaner\b0\par\par\uc2 By Collin Eaton and Benoit Morenne \par \uc2 897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  is slashing 2,000 jobs worldwide, it said Tuesday, the oil industry's latest mass layoff as companies adapt to anemic oil prices and get more efficient at extracting fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 The oil giant's layoffs, amounting to about 3% of its global workforce, culminate a yearslong push to consolidate offices and thin its ranks as it targets billions of dollars in annual structural costs. \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's head count fell about 19% to 61,000 between the end of 2014 and the end of last year, regulatory filings show.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Exxon\uc2  said consolidating workspaces "drives innovation."\par \par \uc2 "Our global office network was established decades ago under very different circumstances," an \uc2 Exxon\uc2  spokeswoman said by email Tuesday. "To support the collaboration so critical to our success, we are aligning our global footprint with our operating model and bringing our teams together."\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , \uc2 ConocoPhillips\uc2  and \uc2 BP\uc2  earlier this year all announced layoffs that will cut thousands of jobs. The companies have said they expect the reductions to yield billions of dollars in savings. Notably, the reductions aren't expected to affect the volumes of oil and gas that the companies produce.\par \par \uc2 The job-shedding will further shrink the industry's rank and file after a yearslong diet of layoffs, attrition and corporate restructuring that has made Big Oil much leaner.\par \par \uc2 Between the end of 2014 and the end of last year, \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's workforce shrunk by more than 26% to about 45,000 employees. Over the same period, \uc2 ConocoPhillips\uc2  reduced total head count by about 38% to around 11,800. At \uc2 BP\uc2 , on the other hand, the average number of employees increased by more than 7% to 91,000.\par \par \uc2 All told, the number of U.S. workers in oil-and-gas extraction has dropped by nearly 80,000 since 2015, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Over that same period, U.S. oil production has climbed 45% to a record of about 13.6 million barrels a day, according to the Energy Department.\par \par \uc2 "These companies are clearly the best in terms of the application of technology, the best in terms of intellectual property, intellectual capital," said Ed Hirs, an energy fellow at the \uc2 University of Houston\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The oil industry's workforce in the U.S. peaked in size in 2014 just above 200,000, when oil fetched more than $100 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 That year, the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  decided to relinquish its role as a moderating force on crude prices, allowing prices in 2015 and 2016 to plunge to levels low enough to send scores of American drillers into bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude prices on Tuesday hovered near $62 a barrel, down from about $80 in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 The industry's head count has never come close to making a full recovery, as U.S. shale companies have found ways to pump more oil with fewer people. In the Permian Basin of West Texas and New Mexico, drillers that once spent more than two weeks drilling a well can now drill horizontal wells extending over nearly 2 miles in just four days.\par \par \uc2 None of \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's job cuts are in the U.S., according to a person familiar with the matter. Almost half the cuts are in Canada, at \uc2 Imperial Oil\uc2 , majority-owned by \uc2 Exxon\uc2 . The affiliate plans to reduce its workforce by 20% by the end of 2027.\par \par \uc2 Mass layoffs are rare for \uc2 Exxon\uc2 . In 2020, as world oil demand took a historic downturn following the onset of the pandemic, the company said it would cut up to 15% of its global workforce, including 1,900 in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 That year, it booked a $20 billion annual loss as prices plummeted.\par \par \uc2 Since 2019, \uc2 Exxon\uc2  has cut about $13.5 billion in structural costs by combining offices and trimming jobs, among other measures. The current cuts extend a corporate redesign that began in the U.S. a few years ago and is now affecting operations in other parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 The sector is in a far better financial shape than it was during previous drops in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has demanded that companies pay down debt and return cash to investors in the form of buybacks and dividends, and drillers have healthier balance sheets that can better weather price swings.\par \par \uc2 But job and budget cuts that target U.S. operations have some worried about potential ripple effects across the rest of the industry. Large players hire drilling and fracking crews, which in turn provide a livelihood to an ecosystem of smaller services providers.\par \par \uc2 "When \uc2 Conoco\uc2  stops spending money and lays off people, there's a lot of other companies that are impacted by that down the food chain," said Roe Patterson, co-founder of private-equity firm Marauder Capital and the former chief executive of \uc2 Basic Energy Services\uc2 , an oil-field services firm that has filed for bankruptcy twice in the past 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Clint Concord, senior vice president of operations at Byrd Oilfield Services, said big fossil-fuel producers want energy-service providers like his to use as few as a third as many people on some oil-field jobs, such as installing well casing.\par \par \uc2 Companies like his are replacing a good deal of manual work with robotic tools, he said. "It's a new era."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251001ela10001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
