\par\par \b \uc2 Walmart\uc2  CEO Issues Wake-Up Call on AI\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BENTONVILLE, Ark. -- \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives aren't sugarcoating the message: Artificial intelligence will wipe out some jobs and reshape the company's workforce.\par \par \uc2 Now the country's largest private employer is making plans to confront that reality.\par \par \uc2 "It's very clear that AI is going to change literally every job," Chief Executive \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2  said last week in one of the most pointed assessments to date from a big-company CEO on AI's likely impact on employment.\par \par \uc2 His remarks reflect a rapid shift from just months ago in how business leaders discuss the potential human cost of the technology. Companies including Ford, \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have bluntly predicted job losses associated with AI. Some have advised other employers to prepare their workforces for change.\par \par \uc2 Some jobs and tasks at the retail juggernaut will be eliminated, while others will be created, McMillon said last week at \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's headquarters here during a workforce conference with executives from other companies. "Maybe there's a job in the world that AI won't change, but I haven't thought of it."\par \par \uc2 Inside \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , top executives have started to examine AI's implications for its workforce in nearly every high-level planning meeting. Company leaders say they are tracking which job types decrease, increase and stay steady to gauge where additional training and preparation can help workers.\par \par \uc2 "Our goal is to create the opportunity for everybody to make it to the other side," McMillon said.\par \par \uc2 For now, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  executives say the transformation means the size of its global workforce will stay roughly flat even as its revenue climbs. It plans to maintain its head count of around 2.1 million global workers over the next three years, but the mix of those jobs will change significantly, said \uc2 Donna Morris\uc2 , \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's chief people officer. What the composition will look like remains murky.\par \par \uc2 "We've got to do our homework, and so we don't have those answers," Morris said.\par \par \uc2 Already \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has built chat bots, which it calls "agents," for customers, suppliers and workers. It is also tracking an expanding share of its supply chain and product trends with AI. In July, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  hired an executive from \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , \uc2 Daniel Danker\uc2 , to oversee those ambitions. Danker reports to McMillon. Part of his role includes working with Morris to determine how \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's workforce should shift.\par \par \uc2 Some changes are rippling across the workforce. In recent years, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has automated many of its warehouses with the help of AI-related technology, triggering some job cuts, executives said. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  is also looking to automate some back-of-store tasks.\par \par \uc2 New roles have been established, too. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , for example, created an "agent builder" position in August -- an employee who builds AI tools to help merchants. It expects to add people in areas like home delivery or in high-touch customer positions, such as its bakeries. The company has also added more in-store maintenance technicians and truck drivers in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Across the industry, the pace of change will be gradual, McMillon said. For example, customer-service tasks in call centers and through online chat functions will become more AI-dependent soon and other tasks not, McMillon said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the corporate world, top executives are pushing their companies to wholeheartedly embrace AI -- and quickly. Some have created internal "heat maps" to decipher which roles or tasks could be automated by AI. Others have pushed staffers to propose new projects.\par \par \uc2 At the agricultural company \uc2 Syngenta\uc2 , for example, the company has identified projects in areas like research and development and supply-chain functions that are ripe for an AI overhaul. Teams will regularly present their findings to CEO \uc2 Jeff Rowe\uc2 . The idea is to spotlight new approaches and identify emerging leaders who seem best equipped to embrace the technology and guide teams through it, he said.\par \par \uc2 "AI is just starting to ripple through the job market," said Ronnie Chatterji, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chief economist, at the Bentonville conference. "I think 18 to 36 months, you're going to see a lot more impact." This month, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  unveiled a partnership with \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and other companies to design an AI-training certificate program.\par \par \uc2 The drumbeat of warnings about AI-related job cuts has increased in recent months. \uc2 Accenture\uc2  CEO \uc2 Julie Sweet\uc2  told investors on Thursday that the firm is "exiting" employees who can't be retrained for the AI age. Meanwhile, it will continue to hire people who are generative AI-fluent and retrain existing workers to serve clients in consulting and other divisions.\par \par \uc2 "Artificial intelligence is going to replace literally half of all white-collar workers in the U.S.," \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said this summer.\par \par \uc2 At the Bentonville confab, executives from \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  and other employers sought to emphasize that, with the right training opportunities, employees could weather the AI storm. Essential human qualities, like the ability to connect with others and develop meaningful relationships, would become even more important, they said.\par \par \uc2 "There's nothing soft about soft skills," said Courtney della Cava, senior managing director and global head of portfolio talent and organizational performance at \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 . The private-equity firm said it has found that an ability to withstand change and evolve is a key quality among the top-performing leaders at its portfolio companies.\par \par \uc2 "Resilience is the No. 1 predictor of success," she said.\par \par \uc2 Though there is plenty of anxiety among workers and leaders, many executives say the U.S. labor market remains healthy and they don't anticipate widespread unemployment because of AI.\par \par \uc2 "I think the history of technology innovation is that people have re-skilled and have found gainful employment in other aspects of the economy," said \uc2 Joe Baratta\uc2 , global head of \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 's private-equity business.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Our Moonshot Moment on AI" -- WSJ Oct. 6, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250929el9t00027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
