\par\par \b \uc2 Volvo Pivots to U.S. Production --- Swedish automaker plans to make top-selling U.S. vehicle in South Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Keilman \par \uc2 796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RIDGEVILLE, S.C. -- \uc2 Volvo Car\uc2 's only U.S. factory was supposed to bring 4,000 jobs to this area 30 miles northwest of Charleston. Today half that number run a single daily shift building expensive electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 That is about to change thanks, in part, to the Trump administration's tariffs. Starting next year the Swedish automaker will crank up production of its most popular SUV in America -- the XC60 -- at this $1.3 billion plant set among the swamps and evergreen trees of the South Carolina low country.\par \par \uc2 For years that model has been shipped to the U.S. from Sweden, but the new 15% levy on imports makes it worthwhile to move its manufacturing to the U.S., Chief Executive \uc2 Hakan Samuelsson\uc2  said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Volvo's XC60 SUV comes in two versions, a standard gasoline-electric hybrid and a more-expensive plug-in hybrid. Combined, the two have notched more than 27,000 sales in the U.S. so far this year, a nearly 20% increase over 2024.\par \par \uc2 The company is adding more hybrid models to the Ridgeville factory because American car buyers proved slow to give up their gas-powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 "We don't want to force anybody into something which they don't want, so we will have more conventional cars and hybrids," Samuelsson said.\par \par \uc2 But he still believes the global future is electric.\par \par \uc2 Even as many American and European automakers and their suppliers are delaying, downsizing or canceling EV projects, Chinese automakers are gobbling up an ever-larger share of the global market.\par \par \uc2 In the longer term, "the growth will be in electric vehicles," Samuelsson said. "If you don't realize that and just nostalgically stay in a shrinking segment, then you have a big risk of not surviving."\par \par \uc2 To that end, Volvo is trying to improve a vehicle it already makes at the Ridgeville plant -- an $81,000 electric SUV called the EX90. Only 2,500 have been sold in the U.S. so far this year, putting it in sixth place among the seven models Volvo sells here.\par \par \uc2 To better compete with rivals, the version coming out next year will have new features, including a safety alert for slippery roads and parallel parking assistance.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the SUV's charging time will get shorter, Volvo said, gaining more than 150 miles of range in 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Volvo also plans to launch a next-generation hybrid designed for American drivers. Executives gave few details about the coming vehicle, but said it would be in production at Ridgeville before 2030.\par \par \uc2 That is the same year Volvo has pledged to make at least 90% of its U.S. fleet "electrified," a term that includes gas-electric hybrids as well as fully electric vehicles, and finally employ 4,000 workers on site as the factory adds new production lines.\par \par \uc2 Ridgeville was built to make 150,000 vehicles a year, but the consulting firm AutoForecast Solutions estimates it runs at about 20% capacity. Auto plants typically need to run at 80% of capacity to be profitable.\par \par \uc2 Volvo has never been a major player in the U.S., with its premium-priced cars claiming just 1% of the market. The brand has loyal fans and has long played up its safety bona fides -- its television commercials once showed crash test dummies withstanding slow-motion collisions -- but Jessica Caldwell, auto analyst for Edmunds, said that reputation means little to younger buyers.\par \par \uc2 Under the majority ownership of China's \uc2 Zhejiang Geely Holding Group\uc2 , which bought Volvo from \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  in 2010, the company has tried to project an image Caldwell calls "the cool technology route." U.S. sales have climbed over the past decade, and the U.S. now accounts for 20% of Volvo's revenue.\par \par \uc2 Samuelsson said it is important for Volvo to have an industrial presence in the U.S. as a global manufacturer, and added that President Trump's tariffs on foreign-made autos and parts also influenced the decision to revamp the Ridgeville plant.\par \par \uc2 Six of the seven models Volvo sells in the U.S. are imported from Europe, which makes them subject to a 15% tariff that adds thousands of dollars to each vehicle's cost. Before Trump returned to office with an aggressive new trade policy, the cars faced a 2.5% tariff.\par \par \uc2 Producing vehicles in South Carolina allows Volvo to avoid the new higher levy. And under the preliminary terms of the U.S. trade deal with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the company can also export vehicles back to Europe tariff-free.\par \par \uc2 "That helps us to live with the 15%," Samuelsson said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
