\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Senators Query Big Tech About H-1B Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anvee Bhutani \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  won approval for more than 14,000 H-1B visas in fiscal 2025. A Sept. 26 U.S. News article about the visas for foreign workers incorrectly said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  won approval for more than 14,000 new hires with H-1B visas.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Oct. 3, 2025)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Big U.S. companies are facing heightened scrutiny from Capitol Hill over their use of foreign employees on H-1B visas, with lawmakers raising concerns about layoffs and slumping job prospects for Americans.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Judiciary Committee Chairman \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  (R., Iowa) and ranking member Dick Durbin (D., Ill.) sent letters to the country's largest users of H-1B visas -- including \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  -- asking them to explain why they continue to hire thousands of H-1B visa holders while also cutting other jobs.\par \par \uc2 The senators asked for detailed information on how many H-1B workers the companies employ, what wages they are paid, and whether American workers have been displaced in the process. The letters cited reporting by The Wall Street Journal about the weak tech job market.\par \par \uc2 The move comes less than a week after President Trump announced an overhaul of the H-1B program, including a new $100,000 fee for companies sponsoring foreign workers. That move caused panic for some companies worried they could retroactively be on the hook for fee payments. They mobilized human-resources teams and lawyers to address employee concerns and ensure compliance.\par \par \uc2 The H-1B system, created in 1990, is the primary pathway for foreign professionals to work in the U.S. Roughly 700,000 people currently live in the country on H-1B visas, according to a National Foundation for American Policy analysis of government data, with technology firms among the biggest users. Most H-1B holders are from India and China.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , the largest H-1B sponsor in the U.S., won approval for more than 14,000 new hires on the visa in fiscal 2025 -- the most of any company -- even as it has announced layoffs affecting tens of thousands of jobs in recent years.\par \par \uc2 "With all of the homegrown American talent relegated to the sidelines, we find it hard to believe that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  cannot find qualified American tech workers to fill these positions," the senators wrote to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aside from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , the other companies receiving letters included \uc2 Deloitte\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Cognizant Technology Solutions\uc2  and \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2 . The companies were asked to respond by Oct. 10.\par \par \uc2 Trump's announcement has already rattled American businesses. In the days after the policy was unveiled, companies scrambled to assess its reach. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  were among the companies that advised H-1B employees to remain in the U.S. or return immediately, fearing they could be barred from re-entry or hit with unexpected costs.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  declined to comment. The other companies didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 To hire H-1B workers, employers have to attest they will pay them the same wage as other workers with similar experience and qualifications, or the prevailing wage in the field in which they will be working.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250926el9q0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*