\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to Slash Office Jobs, Close Stores\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heather Haddon \par \uc2 854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is laying off 900 more corporate workers and closing hundreds of stores, the company's latest moves to trim costs and plow money into improving its cafes.\par \par \uc2 The Seattle-based coffee chain said Thursday that most of the affected positions were located in North America. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it is also eliminating many open positions. The company plans to notify affected workers Friday and asked corporate employees to work from home the rest of this week.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs are the second round that the coffee giant has undertaken since Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  took the helm roughly a year ago. Niccol has said the reductions are needed to direct more resources toward its stores, where he is boosting staff and introducing new training for baristas to make cafes more hospitable.\par \par \uc2 "These steps are to reinforce what we see is working and prioritize our resources against them," Niccol said in a message to employees Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it would spend around $1 billion in severance payments, exiting leases and other costs. The company will begin the process of closing most of the stores in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 The locations that will be closed include cafes in Seattle, among them the chain's flagship Roastery in the city and Reserve store at its headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is pushing to revive its business after six straight quarters of declining same-store sales. Niccol is trying to boost business in cafes while streamlining the company's operations. The chain is grappling with rising labor costs, in addition to surging coffee prices.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's announcement is the latest sign of a cooling U.S. labor market. Companies including Intel, Merck, \uc2 ConocoPhillips\uc2  and others have recently announced layoffs, while others are combining jobs or keeping positions vacant. Some companies have also stepped up in-office requirements, resulting in some workers leaving their jobs for more flexible employment.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, many large companies had been able to pare their workforces without resorting to layoffs, relying on retirements and opting not to fill roles when workers leave for other jobs. That natural attrition is slowing because more workers, hamstrung by declining job openings and spooked by fears of an economic downturn, are staying put.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, job openings have fallen precipitously, from 12.1 million in March 2022 to 7.2 million this past July. Still, there are signs that the U.S. economy is powering through a cloudy employment picture.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had around 10,000 U.S. corporate employees as of September 2024. Those employees work in roles including store development, roasting, manufacturing, warehousing and distribution.\par \par \uc2 In February, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it would lay off 1,100 corporate employees and not fill hundreds of open jobs. Those cuts included many technology workers, including application developers and engineers, according to state records. Some product managers and administrative assistants were laid off, too.\par \par \uc2 After Niccol announced the first round of layoffs, he said he didn't anticipate additional reductions, but it would depend on the business being successful going forward.\par \par \uc2 "I'll be transparent with you, if we don't perform, it becomes a challenging environment. You've got to assess all alternatives," Niccol said during an internal town hall in March.\par \par \uc2 In July, the company announced a voluntary exit program for North American corporate employees. The pay packages ranged from $20,000 to $100,000, according to a message viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Niccol has stepped up in-office work requirements for North American corporate employees to four days a week from three previously. The requirement goes into effect in October.\par \par \uc2 Improving the operations of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores has also been a focus among executives. Niccol is trimming the number of different store formats \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  uses and scaling back the installation of costly food and cold-drink-station equipment.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the company reviewed the chain's stores and found some that it couldn't run profitably or that didn't meet its quality standards, Niccol said Thursday. Those locations have been closed or will be shortly.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would have 18,300 total company and licensed North American locations as of next month, down about 1% from last year when accounting for openings and closings.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it would offer transfers to baristas to nearby locations where possible after closing their stores, and severance packages for those it can't immediately place.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United said it would negotiate with the company to help affected baristas in the several hundred cafes the union represents.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it aims to start adding stores over the next year and renovate more than 1,000 locations to try to improve their atmosphere. Niccol said Thursday that customers so far were visiting the renovated stores more often and staying longer.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has made numerous changes to its C-suite since Niccol joined the company. On Monday, the company said its chief technology officer, \uc2 Deb Hall Lefevre\uc2 , who joined \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in 2022, planned to retire.\par \par \uc2 The company is now searching for a new technology head, according to a message viewed by the Journal.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngbl