\par\par \b \uc2 RV Maker Thor Logs Profit Jump --- Company predicts sales below analyst views, citing tough economic backdrop\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas G. Miller \par \uc2 343 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thor Industries\uc2  posted a higher fourth-quarter profit and better-than-expected sales, but guided for fiscal 2026 sales below analysts' expectations, saying it expects a challenging macroeconomic environment for the year.\par \par \uc2 "While consumer spending and wage growth have been resilient, there is mounting household debt and signs that the labor market is weakening amidst a backdrop of falling interest rates," the RV company said. "Moreover, consumer sentiment and inflation expectations remain volatile."\par \par \uc2 The company guided for 2026 sales of $9 billion to $9.5 billion, and earnings of $3.75 to $4.25 a share. Analysts had forecast sales of $9.63 billion and earnings of $4.12 a share.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose 6.1% to $108.49 on Wednesday, and are now up 13% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Thor\uc2  said affordability concerns were weighing on consumers and that it was trying to mitigate pricing pressures exacerbated by tariffs. "Affordability remains one of the most significant challenges that we face in the marketplace," the company said. "Our operating companies are working tirelessly to prevent any incremental costs from being passed on to the consumer, but in some cases it is unavoidable."\par \par \uc2 The company said it had developed more-affordable private-label products in its motorized RV segment that were resonating with consumers. \uc2 Thor\uc2  reported a 4.6% decline in sales of North American towable RVs but a 7.8% increase in sales of North American motorized RVs.\par \par \uc2 "We believe strongly in our ability to continually identify opportunities that address affordability so as to keep customers engaged with the RV market and foster future sales growth," the company said.\par \par \uc2 The company reported fourth-quarter net income of $125.8 million, or $2.36 a share, up from $90 million, or $1.68 a share, the year prior. Analysts had expected $1.23 a share.\par \par \uc2 Sales were $2.52 billion, down from $2.53 billion the year before. Wall Street had expected $2.32 billion.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250925el9p0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
