\par\par \b \uc2 Markets & Finance: Departing India Can Be a Chore, GM Saga Shows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shan Li \par \uc2 1,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI -- For \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , leaving India has been an odyssey spanning years.\par \par \uc2 The American automaker stopped selling cars in the country in 2017 and said in 2020 it was closing its last factory there. But only last year did GM extricate itself -- for the most part -- from India.\par \par \uc2 Its saga is a reminder of how difficult it remains to do business there. The retreat from India was part of a strategy to shrink the company's global presence and focus on profitable markets, analysts said. GM ran into headaches almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, around 1,100 employees at one factory countered what GM had thought was a generous severance package with lawsuits alleging labor and pay violations. The company also faced tax disputes and the knock-on effects of a geopolitical falling out between India and China.\par \par \uc2 Labor laws required GM to pay its factory workers about a year's salary after firing them, in addition to any severance package it offered. A court also ruled the company had to keep paying the workers half their salaries until the disputes were resolved, though GM appealed that decision and won.\par \par \uc2 "We didn't expect that it would go so bad from the start," said Prajot Gaonkar, former head of employee relations at GM in India.\par \par \uc2 Global companies looking to build factories in India appreciate the country's vast workforce and its potential as a consumer market, but they are often deterred by bureaucracy. The obstacles aren't just about setting up a business -- where officials have recently tried to smooth the path -- but also about closing one down if it doesn't pan out. Regulations make layoffs difficult, politicians are often loath to see investors leave and courts can issue wildly contrary rulings even when the facts are similar.\par \par \uc2 "Exit barriers are a key reason for India's underdeveloped manufacturing sector," said Shoumitro Chatterjee, co-author of a new paper on Indian manufacturing and an assistant professor of international economics at \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As India faces fresh economic pressure from U.S. tariffs of 50%, some say it is more pressing than ever for it to fix roadblocks that hamper its economic potential.\par \par \uc2 In India, it takes an average of 4.3 years to fully close a factory, according to government data cited in the paper Chatterjee co-wrote. That compares with one year in Singapore, 15 months in Germany and one to two years in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 GM's exit was part of a strategic decision to scale back money-losing international operations to invest in electric vehicles, analysts said. The company had lost over $1 billion over two decades of operating in the country, according to legal filings.\par \par \uc2 The sale of one plant, in the western Indian state of Gujarat, went off relatively smoothly in 2017 because the company offered to transfer workers to its other factory in Pune, according to former executives. Unloading the second plant took four years.\par \par \uc2 GM first attempted to sell the Pune factory to Chinese automaker \uc2 Great Wall Motors\uc2 . That fell apart after New Delhi refused to grant permission following a deadly border conflict with China in June 2020 that soured relations.\par \par \uc2 GM shut down production in December of that year and applied for permission to close that factory. The state labor minister promptly rejected the request, declaring that the company could absorb its losses and stage a comeback.\par \par \uc2 The office of the Maharashtra labor commissioner said the law required the state government to refer the dispute to an industrial tribunal once workers raised allegations that GM was trying to skirt labor protections.\par \par \uc2 The state is committed to fostering a business-friendly environment, said the office, adding that following established law for plant closures is part of a predictable investment climate.\par \par \uc2 The employee union declined to negotiate, executives said, instead filing petition after petition with the courts.\par \par \uc2 Gaonkar, who handled employee relations at GM in India, said the hostility was especially shocking because the company had a good working relationship with its employees and offered a package including 110 days of salary for every year of service. But the union demanded that GM either reopen the factory and rehire all the workers, guarantee a new owner would provide them all with jobs or offer a severance package that paid full-time salaries and medical benefits until retirement age, Gaonkar said.\par \par \uc2 The labor disputes scared away several interested buyers, executives said. GM tried to get every worker to waive the right to sue in exchange for compensation. That plan failed.\par \par \uc2 The Industrial Tribunal in Pune finally ruled in 2023 that GM had the right to close down. The union appealed to the Bombay High Court, and several members went on a highly publicized hunger strike along with politicians. The Bombay High Court upheld the industrial tribunal's decision six months later.\par \par \uc2 The union finally came to the negotiating table and agreed to withdraw all its lawsuits in exchange for the severance package that GM had offered right from the start, executives said.\par \par \uc2 Sandeep Bhegade, former union president for the GM factory in Pune, said the automaker had always treated its workers well. But the union fought the closure because its members doubted they could easily find another well-paying factory job.\par \par \uc2 "Now most of us are doing work like driving rickshaws and taxis," he said.\par \par \uc2 South Korean automaker Hyundai, which makes cars in India both for domestic sales and for export, said shortly after the court decision that it had purchased GM's factory.\par \par \uc2 The Maharashtra government said GM's case "should not be viewed as a cautionary tale about regulatory hurdles," and said the length of time it took to close the GM factory wasn't due simply to labor rules. A lot of time was lost, it said, because of geopolitical issues that led to the collapse of the planned sale to \uc2 Great Wall Motors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For over two years, the fate of the plant was tied to a commercial transaction that was stalled at the national and international level," said the office of the state labor commissioner.\par \par \uc2 The whole calculus can change if the local government backs a company's departure, experts said.\par \par \uc2 When Ford said in 2021 that it was shutting down its two plants in India, conglomerate \uc2 Tata\uc2  purchased its factory in Gujarat, a transaction that was relatively smooth because the Indian company agreed to take on all the workers. In southern Tamil Nadu state, workers filed several petitions to try to stop the closure of Ford's Chennai factory.\par \par \uc2 Arun Roy, Tamil Nadu's industry secretary, said the state government came out in support of Ford in the courts, granted it permission to close and allowed the company to remove some machinery.\par \par \uc2 "There's nothing to be gained in bitter partings," he added. Easing the path for departing companies also preserves goodwill for any future deals, Roy said.\par \par \uc2 Ford retained ownership of its Chennai plant, Roy said, to keep a foothold in India. The automaker said last year it planned to restart manufacturing for export at the factory.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250922el9m0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
