\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: U.S. Blocks Shutdown Of Illinois Steel Plant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Trump administration flexed its new authority over \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , blocking the company's plan to shut down production at an Illinois plant this fall.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  notified workers in Granite City, Ill., that plant operations would cease in November. The company, owned by Tokyo-based \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 , said it would continue paying the mill's nearly 800 employees, even without them doing regular production work.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Secretary \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  got wind of the plan and called \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Dave Burritt\uc2 , said a person familiar with the matter. Lutnick told Burritt the administration wouldn't allow operations to stop, and the president would invoke his so-called golden-share authority over plant operations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  cleared \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's $14.1 billion takeover of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  in June after the company entered into a national-security agreement. Conditions of that agreement gave President Trump and future presidents the right to veto plant closings, the transfer of production out of the country and other changes.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  said it has reversed its plan and that Granite City Works would continue rolling steel slabs into sheet steel. "Our goal was to maintain flexibility, and we are pleased to have found a solution to continue to slab consumption at Granite City," the company said.\par \par \uc2 The intervention shows the Trump administration's growing influence in the private sector. Last month, Trump said the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  would take a 10% stake in chip manufacturer Intel, which had received billions of dollars in grants via the 2022 Chips Act.\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2  separately agreed to pay the Trump administration a portion of sales from artificial-intelligence chips to China.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250920el9k0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
